ubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/90b46875bf3ef7e2ab8f5752171009615f030bd8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T16:17:45Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T16:17:45Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(auth): ask the name of whoever came in without one (#92)\n\nSigning in without a password is about to create the account from a verified\ncode (`cygnus-api` #35), and the name cannot be collected on the way in: a form\nthat asks for a name only when the address is unknown answers out loud the\nquestion the whole assisted flow is built to refuse — whether that address has\nan account here. After the session exists, asking reveals nothing.\n\nSo it is a gate, not a form field, and it is **inert for every account that has\na name**, which today is all of them. That is deliberate and is why it ships\nfirst: the API can follow without anybody ever being greeted as \"Boa noite, !\"\nin the gap between two deploys. Same shape as LegalAcceptanceGate, which is\nnested outside it — whether we may process this person''s data is settled before\nwe ask them anything else.\n\nWhile the answer is unknown, the app renders. A hiccup on `GET /auth/me` must\nnot stand between someone and a health record they already have.\n\nThe toast on the request step now depends on the flow. Passwordless says \"we\nsent a code to your e-mail\", because after #35 that is unconditionally true;\npassword reset keeps \"if that address has an account\", which is still exactly\nright for it — there is nothing to send an address with no account, and the\nhedge is what stops the sentence from answering the enumeration question. The\nwording differs by flow, never by address.\n\nOnly the name goes up in the PATCH: `/users/me` demands the current password\nwhenever the e-mail changes, and a payload that always carries the e-mail is one\nrefactor away from tripping that.\n\n261 unit tests, 91 stories, lint, tsc -b, a full build and 8 E2E specs green.","tree":{"sha":"dcba05fd07013eb64230485ca5874f6095df36cc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/dcba05fd07013eb64230485ca5874f6095df36cc"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/90b46875bf3ef7e2ab8f5752171009615f030bd8","verification":{"payload":"tree dcba05fd07013eb64230485ca5874f6095df36cc\nparent a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788797865 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788797865 -0300\n\nfeat(auth): ask the name of whoever came in without one (#92)\n\nSigning in without a password is about to create the account from a verified\ncode (`cygnus-api` #35), and the name cannot be collected on the way in: a form\nthat asks for a name only when the address is unknown answers out loud the\nquestion the whole assisted flow is built to refuse — whether that address has\nan account here. After the session exists, asking reveals nothing.\n\nSo it is a gate, not a form field, and it is **inert for every account that has\na name**, which today is all of them. That is deliberate and is why it ships\nfirst: the API can follow without anybody ever being greeted as \"Boa noite, !\"\nin the gap between two deploys. Same shape as LegalAcceptanceGate, which is\nnested outside it — whether we may process this person''s data is settled before\nwe ask them anything else.\n\nWhile the answer is unknown, the app renders. A hiccup on `GET /auth/me` must\nnot stand between someone and a health record they already have.\n\nThe toast on the request step now depends on the flow. Passwordless says \"we\nsent a code to your e-mail\", because after #35 that is unconditionally true;\npassword reset keeps \"if that address has an account\", which is still exactly\nright for it — there is nothing to send an address with no account, and the\nhedge is what stops the sentence from answering the enumeration question. The\nwording differs by flow, never by address.\n\nOnly the name goes up in the PATCH: `/users/me` demands the current password\nwhenever the e-mail changes, and a payload that always carries the e-mail is one\nrefactor away from tripping that.\n\n261 unit tests, 91 stories, lint, tsc -b, a full build and 8 E2E specs green.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnuOpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAnuQQAFGGqGFjb46wQuc2HdbB/Fnf\nSBLegd74xw03zhLh5pfUXN1QJnPNkWxbKPBoMXBQI0SAr8y43Vz+RP3Qq0MS0Fbi\nNFkhh1V16LPJumAN8T3RLH++tCI5TLAMeZHldZ8VCBQO+QcHYUgp9nQ8PXCRHYfK\nRWe6Y0Q153xjbdI2UZ2EqE3sIkxvqsGCxLERSkH0BmroUn0ya8jf2nO26kdlTf7u\nRpvlurx1G7J1oFqERxz2fvh4RngmxY9gVL0pMtLaXymqX4Sw2kOCnD2H2eFURH+V\n4Q+MdL7nxYJshJiCbanuqPOSV8scnFfqiGr4g6Lfpsokg/C+nVkGaqRNIAEK8Xyp\nOzd4tgpB1lA4NlEZ+sic+9jK3Km5MkkT3m6F2Gd/vQs9Oi5CE94YWsWYeOvAWXyA\nyc72FV2S019VDxp9HehIkRsgistoILpKwZBdjC3Pkc4C3UkcPiqur1vQDp6E2TjC\nQa/kx0/hSTuA60hbBvKiN/JBqidhNqIegzAMbLFErUesJRt4gwuI39mgj8IUwFE4\nG5RNS5F3I0dJuSf46I1mUOfC6mI3nElyCqy1xhCookrzVe8He6SqTkYqSBL5uwYq\nxaxUraq0WxsKWTD+peiYNfhJpEZxH33j0wteovr0FVPjDmkktaUkaZxXb2YS7Z72\ndzzGYEnYrkfyYMYMdz0m\n=yDiv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T16:17:46Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/90b46875bf3ef7e2ab8f5752171009615f030bd8","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKDkwYjQ2ODc1YmYzZWY3ZTJhYjhmNTc1MjE3MTAwOTYxNWYwMzBiZDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765","sha":"a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765"}],"sha":"90b46875bf3ef7e2ab8f5752171009615f030bd8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/90b46875bf3ef7e2ab8f5752171009615f030bd8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T13:14:59Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T13:14:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(nav): the children move into the menu, and the app follows one (#91)\n\n* feat(babies): an age that reads like a person says it\n\nThe app said \"66 meses\" for a five-year-old in all four places it showed an\nage. That was survivable while ages lived inside a card someone had already\nopened. It stops being survivable in a menu that lists the children side by\nside, where the age is the only thing distinguishing two names — which is where\nthis is going next.\n\nThe cut is at two years, because that is where people stop counting in months:\nunder it \"3 meses\", over it \"5 anos e 6 meses\", and the months are dropped at\nzero so a birthday reads \"5 anos\" rather than \"5 anos e 0 meses\".\n\nA hook, not a pure function, because the answer is translated and the plural\nrules are i18next''s. **Zero is plural in Portuguese** and CLDR says otherwise —\n\"0 mês\" is wrong — so the `_zero` form exists to contradict it, the same\ncorrection the old `monthsOld` key already carried.\n\nThe growth table keeps counting months: there the number is the age *at a\nvisit*, plotted against an axis in months, and \"5 anos e 6 meses\" beside a\ncolumn of \"0, 2, 6, 12\" would be the odd one out.\n\n249 tests green.\n\n* feat(nav): the children move into the menu, and the app follows one\n\nDecided by Samuel on 07/09/2026, and it reverses the answer he gave on 06/09\nwhen the side menu was built: the children list *is* going in, and picking one\nnarrows the whole app to that child.\n\nThat is the \"active child\" model of the reference design, which this app had\ndeliberately not had — every page aggregated the family and each one carried its\nown row of chips to narrow itself. The chips answered for one page; a menu\nanswers for the app, so the choice had to stop living in the page.\n\n**A third Zustand store, and the rule says a third needs its reason written\ndown.** It is in `selectedBaby.store.ts`: the selection outlives the page it was\nmade on, six routes read it, one menu writes it, and none of it is server state.\nIt survives a reload — a parent who narrowed the app and refreshed did not\nchange their mind — with every storage access wrapped, because an app that will\nnot boot in a private window is a worse failure than a forgotten preference.\n\n**\"Todas as crianças\" is the first row and it stays.** The reference has no such\nthing: there, one child is always selected. Here the family view is the thing\nthis app does that the reference does not, and `null` is an answer rather than\nthe absence of one.\n\nThe switcher is a radio group, not six buttons: exactly one is true at a time,\nand `aria-checked` is what says so. The age is there because in a list of names\nit is what tells two children apart — which is also why it now reads \"5 anos e\n6 meses\" instead of \"66 meses\" (previous commit).\n\n`BabyFilterChips` is deleted; five pages and the dashboard read the store.\n\nTwo things caught while building, both by a gate rather than by review:\n\n- The axe check in the story failed the selected row''s age: white at 80% over\n  `--primary` is 4.1:1, and 12px text owes 4.5:1. Full opacity now.\n- **The dashboard''s milestone counter kept counting the whole family** while the\n  card below it showed one child — the exact \"hero card is one child, counters\n  are six\" failure the code comment warns about, shipped in the same commit that\n  wrote the warning. Found in a screenshot. There is now a test that fails when\n  the counter ignores the selection, confirmed by putting the bug back.\n\n\"Início\" is \"Visão geral\", which is what the section has always been and what\nthe reference calls it.\n\n257 unit tests, 91 stories, lint, tsc -b, a full build and 8 E2E specs green.","tree":{"sha":"9011a0058fbf19e395008d10e9b0d3ae7c1f166a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/9011a0058fbf19e395008d10e9b0d3ae7c1f166a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765","verification":{"payload":"tree 9011a0058fbf19e395008d10e9b0d3ae7c1f166a\nparent e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788786899 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788786899 -0300\n\nfeat(nav): the children move into the menu, and the app follows one (#91)\n\n* feat(babies): an age that reads like a person says it\n\nThe app said \"66 meses\" for a five-year-old in all four places it showed an\nage. That was survivable while ages lived inside a card someone had already\nopened. It stops being survivable in a menu that lists the children side by\nside, where the age is the only thing distinguishing two names — which is where\nthis is going next.\n\nThe cut is at two years, because that is where people stop counting in months:\nunder it \"3 meses\", over it \"5 anos e 6 meses\", and the months are dropped at\nzero so a birthday reads \"5 anos\" rather than \"5 anos e 0 meses\".\n\nA hook, not a pure function, because the answer is translated and the plural\nrules are i18next''s. **Zero is plural in Portuguese** and CLDR says otherwise —\n\"0 mês\" is wrong — so the `_zero` form exists to contradict it, the same\ncorrection the old `monthsOld` key already carried.\n\nThe growth table keeps counting months: there the number is the age *at a\nvisit*, plotted against an axis in months, and \"5 anos e 6 meses\" beside a\ncolumn of \"0, 2, 6, 12\" would be the odd one out.\n\n249 tests green.\n\n* feat(nav): the children move into the menu, and the app follows one\n\nDecided by Samuel on 07/09/2026, and it reverses the answer he gave on 06/09\nwhen the side menu was built: the children list *is* going in, and picking one\nnarrows the whole app to that child.\n\nThat is the \"active child\" model of the reference design, which this app had\ndeliberately not had — every page aggregated the family and each one carried its\nown row of chips to narrow itself. The chips answered for one page; a menu\nanswers for the app, so the choice had to stop living in the page.\n\n**A third Zustand store, and the rule says a third needs its reason written\ndown.** It is in `selectedBaby.store.ts`: the selection outlives the page it was\nmade on, six routes read it, one menu writes it, and none of it is server state.\nIt survives a reload — a parent who narrowed the app and refreshed did not\nchange their mind — with every storage access wrapped, because an app that will\nnot boot in a private window is a worse failure than a forgotten preference.\n\n**\"Todas as crianças\" is the first row and it stays.** The reference has no such\nthing: there, one child is always selected. Here the family view is the thing\nthis app does that the reference does not, and `null` is an answer rather than\nthe absence of one.\n\nThe switcher is a radio group, not six buttons: exactly one is true at a time,\nand `aria-checked` is what says so. The age is there because in a list of names\nit is what tells two children apart — which is also why it now reads \"5 anos e\n6 meses\" instead of \"66 meses\" (previous commit).\n\n`BabyFilterChips` is deleted; five pages and the dashboard read the store.\n\nTwo things caught while building, both by a gate rather than by review:\n\n- The axe check in the story failed the selected row''s age: white at 80% over\n  `--primary` is 4.1:1, and 12px text owes 4.5:1. Full opacity now.\n- **The dashboard''s milestone counter kept counting the whole family** while the\n  card below it showed one child — the exact \"hero card is one child, counters\n  are six\" failure the code comment warns about, shipped in the same commit that\n  wrote the warning. Found in a screenshot. There is now a test that fails when\n  the counter ignores the selection, confirmed by putting the bug back.\n\n\"Início\" is \"Visão geral\", which is what the section has always been and what\nthe reference calls it.\n\n257 unit tests, 91 stories, lint, tsc -b, a full build and 8 E2E specs green.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnrjTCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+3gQACFe5C0emcCkwDdQHVc1UgPq\nNLqrqsK+sCUOezxSju0s2Ypvl0bITcrIC2CjeGkKqNdugSwTXyFKuSoWvtOSX00/\n2ldGOtBYDEZVxWiHif4RcJ0b/NVIjeczbZPbr00bfjCaS379cvPossvdRQpbknZ7\nnezE4bWpn5ddRxU9uw1xWPu8Fbj58iGD3dIsybsvm3dvYe+J9vEGxfk3KODvhnWp\nRzI1Y9JSMUcD1nkeSHcLnBoBhAglRzydkWxObvFl3CGTRduDIfU/bDXzjnTPj6dw\n1HNHZKqdEhps3bK8ysKAeUpy4Pm2FVpT0mK7rr5gv4h04sWhPt+8QV0HCodp7OFp\nCD/nMdRJYUepOhB/AlK1p2Wh4wL0EH/uMr75lPVgeO5oAU6LTq8uW4PPTKk84YkA\ngwWRbAujJ6AB2jk4V8fC/BikA3l7p7KFLXSFgieWpNVedZvFOAnVTXKtKNmqxECk\nZMZfmOKhsFEFYVHpIlSh08PByYfpeppo+LoZah4IXewfcyaVdhoaHFIORJ9zQjuB\nGJzgT2S2uv1Hg8BoKuoMGRkShuL/E5CgpYmtmQ9qNs760rlwRW6wJIZ6FLLbwwLB\nE5GaBAXlYJCmvLJVJBxm/LLOGQPhr7MlwlveRHVHFVLJtnOcMcFHYXfRZYBH3XVQ\nWuHvHzIh4pFSX3cnKr6K\n=k/0O\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T13:14:59Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKGE3MDcxY2JlNWIwZTM4NTdiOTU2MzNiOWI2ZjQ5ZTQwMmE5ZTE3NjU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac","sha":"e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac"}],"sha":"a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/a7071cbe5b0e3857b95633b9b6f49e402a9e1765"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T02:21:43Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T02:21:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(search): drop a memo that memoised nothing (#90)\n\n`useMemo` over `vaccines.perBaby`, `appointments.perBaby` and the rest: every\none of those is a fresh array on each render — the aggregate hooks build them\nwith `babyList.map(...)` every time they run — so the dependency list never\nmatched and the memo recomputed on every render regardless.\n\nRemoved rather than repaired. Making it real means memoising inside five shared\nhooks that every page uses, to save a substring scan over a few hundred rows;\nwhat it costs today is a reader believing something here is expensive.","tree":{"sha":"80c5443d8a29df54ccf5f2156c6aaacb85175c1e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/80c5443d8a29df54ccf5f2156c6aaacb85175c1e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac","verification":{"payload":"tree 80c5443d8a29df54ccf5f2156c6aaacb85175c1e\nparent f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788747703 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788747703 -0300\n\nrefactor(search): drop a memo that memoised nothing (#90)\n\n`useMemo` over `vaccines.perBaby`, `appointments.perBaby` and the rest: every\none of those is a fresh array on each render — the aggregate hooks build them\nwith `babyList.map(...)` every time they run — so the dependency list never\nmatched and the memo recomputed on every render regardless.\n\nRemoved rather than repaired. Making it real means memoising inside five shared\nhooks that every page uses, to save a substring scan over a few hundred rows;\nwhat it costs today is a reader believing something here is expensive.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnh+3CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAU5QQAB6gMf86S+fUH6mXBLdy1axX\nZ+k9I6Z87jJDTxquconWDIx2B7shwrGaSHezKquEz5k13+8DQ+tK5kp/LtRYdbO2\n9LZJ/B+tq9m0POJ8Hu6C56nOlJtvnxpsTzdvSWf/W3CHVCrHg/1hYEoUSNYDv398\nGh6GlA+HfqDZ7OBeph7/VOyIQFW2dy38LzU2+y6PCgKkyYKrW0kgkAnF/GK/ItgS\nGnnjgCbn54t3zpwblGPzMI2lNJsM7KV79p6IMe6piAJTSQUCrRRqtPTRyBwZ3urK\nbKbQrUqxMVLRvGMFTgsG5IRFW4OcshwzySpUVZAtDDOrPPN7EHDtRQOLFzPXRGX4\nFI8non2fWxv8WhG3KhBdB5WvJTw0kikyDlAT2Gxm6XrLOf2sRM0k3VozbeNCb3gH\n1iJ+EGLe1FNaVioHBqAdRkWSa4x8iWPgg5JMz1Dv3ON1yZ07jUlxQu+BRLR8QnN8\nMvw8dNzf30qX9pzz5nXo1T2vLNcGjqtwtf88xB31xj+BSu+ZuXFbkV/bnt7YsDjv\nlg9kLI9aBRLIKiT3pwKxRWOagPmxCTBMyNQKIrpWsrKsurfn9B2EEZkWMY5LjC78\n670a4NSg3pf2yvIHCNwERnRl8qnmwFnWr6uXaElWJNlopunDsBKvqYPIHZPkZTBX\nxO0fVPjBr69a/h/TAr5W\n=/huK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T02:21:44Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKGU1MzVkOTFlYzVhYmI5NzYwYmZmNzAwYTU4ZTdhOWM4OGJkMTA3YWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e","sha":"f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e"}],"sha":"e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/e535d91ec5abb9760bff700a58e7a9c88bd107ac"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T02:19:00Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T02:19:00Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(readme): say what the app actually does now (#89)\n\nThe public README still described an app with three features. It has twelve:\nthe intro named vaccines, appointments and milestones, and the structure block\nlisted six feature folders against the twelve on disk — medications,\nprofessionals, the health plan, growth with the WHO band and the global search\nhad all arrived since it was written.\n\nThe script table was missing `test:e2e`, `contract:check` and the two extra\nbuild artifacts, and described `build` as \"typecheck + production build\" when it\nis four builds in a load-bearing order.\n\nTwo leftovers of the `gender` → `sexAtBirth` rename go too:\n`babies.schemas.test.ts` was still passing `gender: ''MALE''` to a form schema\nthat has not had the field since #82. It changed nothing — the schema ignores\nunknown keys — which is exactly why it survived, and why it was misleading to\nanyone reading the test to learn the shape.\n\nThis is the same failure the repo has been bitten by three times: a document is\na claim about the past, and the code wins.","tree":{"sha":"accabdea01d4893343f16e7bd5e29bbea3204534","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/accabdea01d4893343f16e7bd5e29bbea3204534"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e","verification":{"payload":"tree accabdea01d4893343f16e7bd5e29bbea3204534\nparent c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788747540 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788747540 -0300\n\ndocs(readme): say what the app actually does now (#89)\n\nThe public README still described an app with three features. It has twelve:\nthe intro named vaccines, appointments and milestones, and the structure block\nlisted six feature folders against the twelve on disk — medications,\nprofessionals, the health plan, growth with the WHO band and the global search\nhad all arrived since it was written.\n\nThe script table was missing `test:e2e`, `contract:check` and the two extra\nbuild artifacts, and described `build` as \"typecheck + production build\" when it\nis four builds in a load-bearing order.\n\nTwo leftovers of the `gender` → `sexAtBirth` rename go too:\n`babies.schemas.test.ts` was still passing `gender: ''MALE''` to a form schema\nthat has not had the field since #82. It changed nothing — the schema ignores\nunknown keys — which is exactly why it survived, and why it was misleading to\nanyone reading the test to learn the shape.\n\nThis is the same failure the repo has been bitten by three times: a document is\na claim about the past, and the code wins.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnh8UCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAGYIQAFPziXkLsiYZ2jtd1Zf0zXjb\nTTUkJDPKR4rx7++Iga4u9FoHC0aVQzavX7f3y+N1UWji0bp24aVgFAD+HHKwCDVe\nLNGSVe/YQgRkVFB16a3kQe4bksjz87zORTB/FRk4nooLXN1qZ6b6YSQo5mVvnU5S\nFTpYbsR6Lsajwi+TdlbYWjhs+Ei/ywqoNPwWLVK6y7rBuDrFPv0brQxIOc9sS60d\nFpzaZB7wUlSX7LMc1OQl/JuXiYICAyRuLR7GcF2PtRCZktLl26eKT4ivRSYfBJc8\n8UHo86l7geDAp1ABFlr6jyIi421MYNsEkpFa6iXDj8/ocpJZnrqgMaQ93T0sQyCG\nV4EGst19tfHGW1eI0kAO6tfFHlxTcTtmD+F621eVOMU1PxhZZSq3oknQnDUUNlST\nuLTYo9mfywtu5nMcJx6JoM2t7YwpPpji6GR/6U/U8s9ghg6skrzkWl5ymOowKScM\nfWHbnNsa9L5pJ+aHMIVuk/mEuHWP7BGg3ixOy82EDAVgcAYH1N+CaL20yMbtZbLD\nNvjNWkEhFGnAuPl0EOaFq7mNs43xWLK5wNrU5nROBT1CDZQVqfORPvbAmyuIUTYD\nr8RMz0sSrPsGS0t5DYXMFzk+F3VqzSmEkW3m+iEl4vm6VolEOL+NqCZBdO/X9J+e\nIHYIPkSOsdjRnt0mVrgU\n=vOBh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T02:19:01Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKGYyNjY5ZjRhNjhmODExNmQyMjU1MjM5NzYxNzZiZjg1YmY1MTNmM2U","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8","sha":"c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8"}],"sha":"f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/f2669f4a68f8116d225523976176bf85bf513f3e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T02:14:49Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T02:14:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(search): a dialog that searches every section (#88)\n\n* feat(search): what a search across the account matches\n\nThe matching half, with no React in it: normalisation, the field lists each\ndomain is searched by, and the grouping.\n\nAccent-folding is not a nicety in pt-BR — \"varicela\" has to find \"Varicela\" and\na parent typing on a phone in a hurry does not hold the accent key down. Both\nsides go through the same function, because normalising only the query is the\nversion that silently fails on the data''s own accents.\n\nTwo characters minimum. One letter matches most of the database and answers\nnothing, and a list flashing on every keystroke of a word being typed is worse\nthan no list.\n\nWhat each domain is searched by is a decision, not a field dump: a vaccine\nmatches on the catalogue''s own `description` and `guidance` too, which is why\n\"hepatite\" finds a dose whose name says only \"Penta\"; a child matches on their\nallergies, which is the thing people look up in a hurry; an appointment matches\non its notes, not only the doctor''s name.\n\nDomains are listed in the menu''s order, deliberately, and not ranked. A parent\nsearching \"amoxicilina\" knows whether they want a prescription or a visit note;\nwhat they cannot do is guess which of six lists a scoring function put first\ntoday.\n\n* feat(search): a dialog that searches every section\n\nThe field the top bar has had a place for since the side menu landed. Wide\nscreens get the field; a phone''s top bar has no room for one, so the same dialog\nopens from an icon — two triggers, one search, because an inline dropdown beside\na phone sheet is how two versions of a feature drift apart.\n\n**It searches what the app already loads, and there is no search endpoint.**\nThat is a safety property before it is a performance one: an endpoint would be a\nsecond implementation of every domain''s visibility rules, which is the class of\nbug that shows one family another family''s data. A result here can only be\nsomething the reader could have reached by walking the app.\n\nThe six aggregate hooks have no `enabled` flag, so the results component is\nmounted only while the dialog is open and the dialog itself is `lazy` — imported\neagerly it dragged six features'' api modules into the entry chunk (+1.8 kB gzip\non first paint for a panel most sessions never open). It is 2.1 kB gzip in its\nown chunk now, fetched on the first click; the entry pays 1.4 kB for the copy in\nthree locales and the two triggers.\n\nEight rows per section, then a link to the section itself: a household of three\nchildren matches the same word across a hundred calendar doses, and what is\nworth seeing past the eighth row is that the word also matched somewhere *else*.\n\n**A failed query never gets called \"nothing found\".** A list that did not load\nmatches nothing, so the honest-looking sentence — \"nada encontrado para\nfernanda\" — is a lie about data that is sitting there. Found by accident: a\nfixture in the test above had a nine-character id, one row failed the parse, the\nwhole response was rejected, and the dialog reported an empty search with a\nstraight face. There is now a test for it, and the same run exposed two other\ntests that had been quietly searching a dead vaccine list because their stub''s\n`metadata` was `null`.\n\n245 unit tests, 94 stories, lint, tsc -b, a full build and 8 E2E specs green —\nthe a11y sweep opens the dialog, scans it, types, and scans the results.","tree":{"sha":"de19722c57b22cdb3948a80f2e44b09aa48fdc73","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/de19722c57b22cdb3948a80f2e44b09aa48fdc73"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8","verification":{"payload":"tree de19722c57b22cdb3948a80f2e44b09aa48fdc73\nparent 0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788747289 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788747289 -0300\n\nfeat(search): a dialog that searches every section (#88)\n\n* feat(search): what a search across the account matches\n\nThe matching half, with no React in it: normalisation, the field lists each\ndomain is searched by, and the grouping.\n\nAccent-folding is not a nicety in pt-BR — \"varicela\" has to find \"Varicela\" and\na parent typing on a phone in a hurry does not hold the accent key down. Both\nsides go through the same function, because normalising only the query is the\nversion that silently fails on the data''s own accents.\n\nTwo characters minimum. One letter matches most of the database and answers\nnothing, and a list flashing on every keystroke of a word being typed is worse\nthan no list.\n\nWhat each domain is searched by is a decision, not a field dump: a vaccine\nmatches on the catalogue''s own `description` and `guidance` too, which is why\n\"hepatite\" finds a dose whose name says only \"Penta\"; a child matches on their\nallergies, which is the thing people look up in a hurry; an appointment matches\non its notes, not only the doctor''s name.\n\nDomains are listed in the menu''s order, deliberately, and not ranked. A parent\nsearching \"amoxicilina\" knows whether they want a prescription or a visit note;\nwhat they cannot do is guess which of six lists a scoring function put first\ntoday.\n\n* feat(search): a dialog that searches every section\n\nThe field the top bar has had a place for since the side menu landed. Wide\nscreens get the field; a phone''s top bar has no room for one, so the same dialog\nopens from an icon — two triggers, one search, because an inline dropdown beside\na phone sheet is how two versions of a feature drift apart.\n\n**It searches what the app already loads, and there is no search endpoint.**\nThat is a safety property before it is a performance one: an endpoint would be a\nsecond implementation of every domain''s visibility rules, which is the class of\nbug that shows one family another family''s data. A result here can only be\nsomething the reader could have reached by walking the app.\n\nThe six aggregate hooks have no `enabled` flag, so the results component is\nmounted only while the dialog is open and the dialog itself is `lazy` — imported\neagerly it dragged six features'' api modules into the entry chunk (+1.8 kB gzip\non first paint for a panel most sessions never open). It is 2.1 kB gzip in its\nown chunk now, fetched on the first click; the entry pays 1.4 kB for the copy in\nthree locales and the two triggers.\n\nEight rows per section, then a link to the section itself: a household of three\nchildren matches the same word across a hundred calendar doses, and what is\nworth seeing past the eighth row is that the word also matched somewhere *else*.\n\n**A failed query never gets called \"nothing found\".** A list that did not load\nmatches nothing, so the honest-looking sentence — \"nada encontrado para\nfernanda\" — is a lie about data that is sitting there. Found by accident: a\nfixture in the test above had a nine-character id, one row failed the parse, the\nwhole response was rejected, and the dialog reported an empty search with a\nstraight face. There is now a test for it, and the same run exposed two other\ntests that had been quietly searching a dead vaccine list because their stub''s\n`metadata` was `null`.\n\n245 unit tests, 94 stories, lint, tsc -b, a full build and 8 E2E specs green —\nthe a11y sweep opens the dialog, scans it, types, and scans the results.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnh4ZCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA744QAJqtQPeC0+A/Q2PF3LzpAfRR\nDXmk44hlltEXv8k+lKHxUZ1YUJQibSDMm3URthY64QYxyCMW9U2RaaQzP6okZxoo\n9YkwIGZwWekM0uw9/snTSUROcKkO5FaV4WfmPgDEEvdqbXjaVIuBtQ+g9wkSv/+M\nA/JdfX3eKosxxA/5GfPzGJIE6fbvpk1XCFxlL7trgocWUExUtnv/WXUK+zQ7Kiz6\noHKHkGlcntZC8r2SjOL6Oyuv5nTQ6DqnLD3D4fG6g2b0Kl/vsOIVl+Mjw8E6H3oI\nHM/T5HOb2SZ7NoCXlpC0DgTPQiYhw09LOoZjvk5CqoacvBATSuQOkqATKMMeH3fK\ngo4PWyhgo2IhD2dMClrUoWMZJ2KtxJ/j3Za1a0+5ZHrPY5rRAUfhDlBkoCXUT1S6\ngGO9I8u6nswroSxBmyXH/YcpdPfRcS3D1knyrGxLJ81uqwLx64DWmPMqsoUHgOPz\nJ6JgTM1f2lluPzMs7gUnhGTbJVrHctd0GILwmz3CJAViO5OYP+d5//iD/R4hNVDT\nVpK7r4ONKh0wtvmmYvOsIL1fCa925MAwo7w0yqCkYuzt9HUEp9xrzFFSpT9rljbV\n75pJICbFrEXCxRfDqUz48Fho3wgTYYr1HFMf+SXfHyUOv5Pyok6D6a8J6GlnWTp9\nxB69LhKJ2AtV8hleZJk0\n=Dywn\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T02:14:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKGMwNDg2ZmRiMjZhNjNlNWU1NzFlNzZlYjNlMTUxYTRmMjhkOTZjZjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181","sha":"0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181"}],"sha":"c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/c0486fdb26a63e5e571e76eb3e151a4f28d96cf8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T01:50:49Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T01:50:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(growth): the WHO reference band behind the child''s curve (#87)\n\n* feat(growth): the WHO reference tables, generated from who.int\n\nThe numbers a parent will compare their child against. A transcription error in\nthem is invisible -- every value is plausible, none throws, and the chart looks\nexactly as right as before -- so they are not typed in: `scripts/build-who-\ngrowth-reference.mjs` downloads the four WHO percentile \"expanded tables\" and\nwrites the generated file, printing the SHA-256 of each source into its header.\nAnyone can re-run it and diff.\n\nThe script has no dependencies, deliberately, so it still runs years from now: a\n.xlsx is a zip of XML and Node ships DEFLATE but no archive reader, so it walks\nthe central directory itself in about forty lines.\n\nScope is 0-5 years, which is what WHO publishes as a table. The 5-19 reference\n(2007) is offered only as PDF charts -- the UI says so rather than drawing a\nband that stops without explaining itself.\n\nSampled every 15 days rather than every day (1857 rows per table) or every month\n(61): the band is steepest in the first months, where monthly sampling visibly\ncorners it. 125 rows per table, four tables, 24.7 kB of source in the lazily\nloaded route chunk.\n\nValues are converted to whole grams and millimetres here, the units the API\nstores and the chart plots, so nothing multiplies by 1000 at render time.\n\nSpot-checked against the published charts: a boy''s median birth weight is\n3346 g, a girl''s 3232 g, a boy''s median birth length 499 mm.\n\n* feat(growth): the WHO band behind the child''s curve\n\n\"Is that normal?\" is the question a growth curve is looked at to answer, and\nuntil now this page could not answer it. P3-P97 as a soft area, P15-P85 darker\ninside it, the median as a hairline -- the shape a caderneta draws.\n\nPainted in `--color-ink` at 8%, twice, so the inner band lands near 15% without\na second colour to keep in contrast, and flips with the theme on its own. Never\nin the indicator''s own colour: a green band under a green line reads as a second\nmeasurement of the same thing.\n\n**The chart clips the table, not the caller.** It is the only thing that knows\nwhere its axis ends, and that end is not the oldest measurement -- a newborn''s\naxis has a one-month floor. A band cut to the child''s age instead stops\nmid-plot with a visible step. One row past the edge is kept so the fill reaches\nthe border.\n\nThe value scale now covers the band as well as the child, which is the whole\npoint of drawing one: a curve sitting above P97 has to *look* like it does, and\nit cannot on an axis that stops at the child''s own maximum. The curve looks\nflatter as a result, which is honest -- the shape was never the message, the\nposition is.\n\nTwo silences, written differently because they are different. No sex at birth on\nthe profile means no band at all: WHO publishes one curve for boys and one for\ngirls, there is no neutral one, and choosing either would invent the comparison\n-- the line says so and links to where it is filled in (the field is optional\nsince #82 and left blank on purpose by people who have their reasons). Past five\nyears the table simply ends, and nothing the reader does changes that.\n\nGated in a story, confirmed able to fail: `fill-ink/8000` -- a class Tailwind\nnever emits -- leaves the SVG default `rgb(0, 0, 0)` with no alpha channel and\nan invisible band under a legend still claiming one. Read as \"has an alpha\",\nwhich handles both `oklab(... / 0.08)` and `rgba()`.\n\nCosts 1.4 kB gzip on the entry chunk (155.725 -> 157.132, the copy in three\nlocales); the tables ride in the lazy route chunk, 11.4 kB gzip all in.\n\n228 unit tests, 94 stories, lint, tsc -b and a full build green.\n\n* fix(growth): the band ends on the axis, not one row past it\n\nKeeping the first row beyond the edge and letting the chart clamp it clamps only\nthe x. That row''s percentiles belong to an older child, so the band jumped\nupward in a visible step exactly at the border — on a newborn''s one-month axis\nit looked like a rendering glitch, which is how it was found: in a capture, not\nin a test.\n\nThe last row is interpolated onto the edge instead, so the fill reaches the\nborder with the values that actually belong there. The test pins both halves:\nthe last age is the axis maximum exactly, and its median sits between the two\ntable rows it was interpolated from.","tree":{"sha":"c987cb2f22e18fa44c0e16fc7dee6ed3e1dedd4b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/c987cb2f22e18fa44c0e16fc7dee6ed3e1dedd4b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181","verification":{"payload":"tree c987cb2f22e18fa44c0e16fc7dee6ed3e1dedd4b\nparent 0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788745849 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788745849 -0300\n\nfeat(growth): the WHO reference band behind the child''s curve (#87)\n\n* feat(growth): the WHO reference tables, generated from who.int\n\nThe numbers a parent will compare their child against. A transcription error in\nthem is invisible -- every value is plausible, none throws, and the chart looks\nexactly as right as before -- so they are not typed in: `scripts/build-who-\ngrowth-reference.mjs` downloads the four WHO percentile \"expanded tables\" and\nwrites the generated file, printing the SHA-256 of each source into its header.\nAnyone can re-run it and diff.\n\nThe script has no dependencies, deliberately, so it still runs years from now: a\n.xlsx is a zip of XML and Node ships DEFLATE but no archive reader, so it walks\nthe central directory itself in about forty lines.\n\nScope is 0-5 years, which is what WHO publishes as a table. The 5-19 reference\n(2007) is offered only as PDF charts -- the UI says so rather than drawing a\nband that stops without explaining itself.\n\nSampled every 15 days rather than every day (1857 rows per table) or every month\n(61): the band is steepest in the first months, where monthly sampling visibly\ncorners it. 125 rows per table, four tables, 24.7 kB of source in the lazily\nloaded route chunk.\n\nValues are converted to whole grams and millimetres here, the units the API\nstores and the chart plots, so nothing multiplies by 1000 at render time.\n\nSpot-checked against the published charts: a boy''s median birth weight is\n3346 g, a girl''s 3232 g, a boy''s median birth length 499 mm.\n\n* feat(growth): the WHO band behind the child''s curve\n\n\"Is that normal?\" is the question a growth curve is looked at to answer, and\nuntil now this page could not answer it. P3-P97 as a soft area, P15-P85 darker\ninside it, the median as a hairline -- the shape a caderneta draws.\n\nPainted in `--color-ink` at 8%, twice, so the inner band lands near 15% without\na second colour to keep in contrast, and flips with the theme on its own. Never\nin the indicator''s own colour: a green band under a green line reads as a second\nmeasurement of the same thing.\n\n**The chart clips the table, not the caller.** It is the only thing that knows\nwhere its axis ends, and that end is not the oldest measurement -- a newborn''s\naxis has a one-month floor. A band cut to the child''s age instead stops\nmid-plot with a visible step. One row past the edge is kept so the fill reaches\nthe border.\n\nThe value scale now covers the band as well as the child, which is the whole\npoint of drawing one: a curve sitting above P97 has to *look* like it does, and\nit cannot on an axis that stops at the child''s own maximum. The curve looks\nflatter as a result, which is honest -- the shape was never the message, the\nposition is.\n\nTwo silences, written differently because they are different. No sex at birth on\nthe profile means no band at all: WHO publishes one curve for boys and one for\ngirls, there is no neutral one, and choosing either would invent the comparison\n-- the line says so and links to where it is filled in (the field is optional\nsince #82 and left blank on purpose by people who have their reasons). Past five\nyears the table simply ends, and nothing the reader does changes that.\n\nGated in a story, confirmed able to fail: `fill-ink/8000` -- a class Tailwind\nnever emits -- leaves the SVG default `rgb(0, 0, 0)` with no alpha channel and\nan invisible band under a legend still claiming one. Read as \"has an alpha\",\nwhich handles both `oklab(... / 0.08)` and `rgba()`.\n\nCosts 1.4 kB gzip on the entry chunk (155.725 -> 157.132, the copy in three\nlocales); the tables ride in the lazy route chunk, 11.4 kB gzip all in.\n\n228 unit tests, 94 stories, lint, tsc -b and a full build green.\n\n* fix(growth): the band ends on the axis, not one row past it\n\nKeeping the first row beyond the edge and letting the chart clamp it clamps only\nthe x. That row''s percentiles belong to an older child, so the band jumped\nupward in a visible step exactly at the border — on a newborn''s one-month axis\nit looked like a rendering glitch, which is how it was found: in a capture, not\nin a test.\n\nThe last row is interpolated onto the edge instead, so the fill reaches the\nborder with the values that actually belong there. The test pins both halves:\nthe last age is the axis maximum exactly, and its median sits between the two\ntable rows it was interpolated from.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnhh5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASmQQALFFxk5rQGMi/9pnu7H7D33j\ndKCRkW6HyA/gY84ng+wgC2MeSONK5e2X66CUR/5tcPC8c7IsdH1orpywBQM+Fqr2\nLHReLkzXAtCYz1inoRsJsn2WyoIOu6S1F/UWLxdr/OlEQsMsMql2A8oWfp26+DCe\nSAg9Pv64WiSTdBGxJQdJyRFloN8oJHn/nsyByO60DpnO3YSXIroTXv7bL+VaYu8m\nfcq5W+swTYVmG3HWEr1sWqi8R9fGYwprjolo9ed4UlzrQTm7/0fu/NPiA4cWI+Tz\ntN0nic7NHT/WHrMSSulo1+UQ3CPs93Bw1ZMm8LQKSAerPNfeLW+zfPNju2Vx+5x7\nHtnzR5hfd75vgRKAdHbldWmpin/49x9YkPfm8Dp0DoYZAcJSwykMoDynSy+7zOtu\n/qbqZpYUUjh9wINBuBpb2viVXCQ/kPhjJCHhLzsCA2pwCR29gXQy2zRTMx1zDjhZ\n+/65b3IBH5Hz3QH0D1mR6TB+tChFoCl9hJ9S1hYXSTDM2s0n4aEawESYY6mdUyMA\nxACp4qIu2+Jei6f+hVL1l9dQMOQchphguI2/8zWLCsQct3r7Zfz2l0dNVbyefa4l\nUhvgz4pswTAuKEYG9i2ov/EOOvsGSxMJMe0gcv/kJL0u0VTaiAokKvMUCp97Yryj\nCbwv2W7U20pQLmdOxaDa\n=W2Uj\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T01:50:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKDBmNDc3NGE3NzhkODQ3ZDVmM2M0NjE0NjM1Zjk2ODZiYmNjOTExODE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7","sha":"0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7"}],"sha":"0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/0f4774a778d847d5f3c4614635f9686bbcc91181"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T01:29:55Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T01:29:55Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(growth): a page for how the child is growing (#86)\n\n* fix(date): a birth date is a calendar date, not a UTC instant\n\n`ageInMonths` needed a sibling that answers for an arbitrary moment, because a\ngrowth curve places a point at the age the child was *at the visit*, not today.\nExtracting it exposed a bug that was already shipping.\n\n`new Date(''2026-01-15'')` parses as UTC midnight, and every reader here asks for\nlocal components. At UTC-3 that Date answers `getDate() === 14`, so a child born\non the 15th counted a new month from the 14th -- one day early, every month,\neverywhere the age is shown. Invisible except on the day it flips, which is why\nnothing caught it. The regression test is the assertion that failed while the\nparse was still `new Date(value)`.\n\nThe fix and the extraction are one commit on purpose: the only test that\ndemonstrates the defect is a test of the new function, and writing one that\ndodged it to keep the commits separate would be theatre.\n\n`ageInMonthsExactAt` comes with them, and disagrees with the other two by design.\nWhole months are right for reading (\"2 meses\") and wrong for placing: two visits\nthree weeks apart in a newborn''s first month both land on 0, and the curve draws\na vertical line at exactly the age where it is steepest and most worth reading.\nDays over 30.4375 keeps them apart, and the two never differ by a whole month.\n\n196 tests green.\n\n* feat(growth): the series a visit leaves behind\n\nNo growth endpoint, and none needed: weight and height are recorded on the\nvisit that took them, so the series *is* the visits read in order. The curve\ntherefore cannot disagree with the appointment list -- they are the same rows.\n\nThree rules, each with a test:\n\n- Only COMPLETED, same admission rule as latestMeasuredVisit and for the same\n  reason: the API will hold a measurement on a cancelled visit, but a cancelled\n  visit did not weigh anybody.\n- Oldest first. The appointment list sorts the other way, which is right for\n  \"what happened recently\" and backwards for something read left to right.\n- A visit that recorded one measure and not the other contributes a point to\n  one indicator only. Interpolating across the gap would draw a line asserting\n  a height that nobody measured; the two indicators having different point\n  counts on the same page is the truth about how the visits went.\n\nValues stay in grams and millimetres all the way to the label: the conversion\nlives in measurements.ts and nowhere else.\n\n* feat(growth): a chart that draws one measure over age\n\nNo chart library. The entry chunk is 138 kB gzip and the lightest of the usual\ncandidates adds a third of that again for one line and two axes; this is a\npolyline, four gridlines and some absolutely-positioned text.\n\nThe geometry is SVG and every word is HTML. `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` lets\nthe plot stretch to the card''s width with no arithmetic and\n`vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` keeps the line 2px through the stretch --\nbut text inside that viewBox would be squashed horizontally and would scale with\nthe container instead of with the reader''s font size. HTML labels positioned by\npercentage answer to browser zoom like the rest of the page (WCAG 1.4.4).\n\nTwo measures, two charts, never two axes: a second y scale makes the crossing\npoint of weight and height look like a fact when it is an artefact of where the\nscales were pinned.\n\nThe axis arithmetic is `niceScale`, tested without a browser: 5.4-15.8 becomes\n5/10/15/20 rather than four equal parts at 8.0, 10.6, 13.2. A single\nmeasurement -- min equal to max -- opens a range around the value instead of\ndividing by zero and printing the same number on every tick.\n\nColours are emerald-700/violet-500, and emerald-600/violet-400 in dark. Not\npicked: run through the palette validator against each surface, where the app''s\ndark `--primary` (#34d399) sits outside the dark lightness band and emerald-600\ndoes not.\n\nThree gates in the story, each confirmed able to fail:\n\n- The points are drawn in order and spread. **Not** \"every dot is inside the\n  plot\" -- `positionIn` clamps, so a scale that does not contain its data piles\n  points on the edge and a bounds check stays green. Measured: shrinking the age\n  scale to a third passed the bounds version and fails this one.\n- A dot is >= 24px to hit (WCAG 2.5.8), which is the span around the 8px mark.\n- The line is painted. `stroke-emerald-750` -- a class Tailwind never emits --\n  leaves `stroke: none`, an invisible curve and no error anywhere.\n\n* feat(growth): a page for how the child is growing\n\n`/crescimento`, the seventh section of the menu, and the first thing the side\nmenu made room for: the label needs ~85px, which the bottom bar never had.\n\nIt reads the appointments and owns no endpoint. Weight and height are recorded\non the visit that took them, so the series *is* the visits -- and the curve can\nnever disagree with the appointment list, because they are the same rows.\nReaching into another feature''s `api/` is the sanctioned shape for exactly this\ncase, and the alternative is a second copy that can drift from the first.\n\nOne section per child, not one chart with a line each. Two children are two\ndifferent ages at the same instant: a shared axis either compares them on the\nsame *date*, which says nothing about growth, or stacks curves whose points\nnever line up. The chips filter which sections show -- the same local filter the\nother aggregate pages use, no global \"selected child\".\n\nUnder each pair of charts, the same numbers as a table. It is not a nicety: it\nis what makes the figures readable to someone who cannot see them, which is what\nlets each chart be a single `role=\"img\"` with no focusable dots, and it is the\nonly place the values are exact -- a curve says \"it went up\", a row says 7,9 kg\non 28/07. A visit that recorded one measure and not the other shows an em dash,\nnever a blank: the blank reads as data lost, the dash as a visit that did not\nmeasure.\n\nThree states that are not the happy one, each with a test: a single measurement\nsays so (\"uma medida sozinha ainda não desenha uma curva\" -- a lone dot on a\nfull axis otherwise looks like the rest failed to load); no measurement at all\nsends the person to /appointments, where measurements are entered, instead of\nleaving a dead end; and a cancelled visit that kept a measurement is not counted.\n\nCosts 2.7 kB gzip on the entry chunk (152.970 -> 155.725 bytes, measured), which\nis the icon, the menu row and the copy in three locales. The page itself is a\nlazy 9.7 kB chunk, like every other route.\n\nTwo stale comments in router.tsx went with it: both argued from the bottom bar''s\nwidth arithmetic, which no longer exists. The conclusion about /plano-de-saude\nsurvives, for a reason that is now about what a section is for rather than about\n320px.\n\nlint, tsc -b, 214 unit tests, 93 stories and a full build green.\n\n* fix(growth): an age axis in whole months, and the visit''s own date\n\nBoth found by looking at a seeded page, which is why the capture script exists;\nneither fails a test that was written before them.\n\nA newborn''s only measurement sits at age ~0, so the age axis had nothing to\ndivide and padded itself in both directions: it printed **-1 month**. It now\ntakes a floor of one month on the span and a whole-month step -- \"0,25 meses\" is\na week, said the wrong way. Weight and height pass no minimum and keep their\ndecimals, which a test pins.\n\nThe table read \"07/09/2025\" beside \"1 mês\" for a visit two months after birth.\n`scheduledAt.slice(0, 10)` is the **UTC** date while the age is counted from the\nlocal one, and for a visit late in the day those are different days. Both the\nrow and the tooltip now use `splitScheduledAt`, the same local date the\nappointment card has always shown.\n\nAnd the reason a test could have missed it: **the suite''s timezone was whatever\nthe machine had** -- UTC-3 here, UTC on the runner. At offset zero neither this\nbug nor the `new Date(''2026-01-15'')` one from earlier today is reproducible at\nall, so the regression tests would have been green decoration in CI. `TZ` is now\npinned to America/Sao_Paulo, which is where this app''s users are. Confirmed by\nrestoring the UTC slice: the new test fails, and it fails on the runner too.\n\n218 tests green.","tree":{"sha":"43828ade26eaa17b48adc57107f09b72ef612367","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/43828ade26eaa17b48adc57107f09b72ef612367"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7","verification":{"payload":"tree 43828ade26eaa17b48adc57107f09b72ef612367\nparent 1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788744595 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788744595 -0300\n\nfeat(growth): a page for how the child is growing (#86)\n\n* fix(date): a birth date is a calendar date, not a UTC instant\n\n`ageInMonths` needed a sibling that answers for an arbitrary moment, because a\ngrowth curve places a point at the age the child was *at the visit*, not today.\nExtracting it exposed a bug that was already shipping.\n\n`new Date(''2026-01-15'')` parses as UTC midnight, and every reader here asks for\nlocal components. At UTC-3 that Date answers `getDate() === 14`, so a child born\non the 15th counted a new month from the 14th -- one day early, every month,\neverywhere the age is shown. Invisible except on the day it flips, which is why\nnothing caught it. The regression test is the assertion that failed while the\nparse was still `new Date(value)`.\n\nThe fix and the extraction are one commit on purpose: the only test that\ndemonstrates the defect is a test of the new function, and writing one that\ndodged it to keep the commits separate would be theatre.\n\n`ageInMonthsExactAt` comes with them, and disagrees with the other two by design.\nWhole months are right for reading (\"2 meses\") and wrong for placing: two visits\nthree weeks apart in a newborn''s first month both land on 0, and the curve draws\na vertical line at exactly the age where it is steepest and most worth reading.\nDays over 30.4375 keeps them apart, and the two never differ by a whole month.\n\n196 tests green.\n\n* feat(growth): the series a visit leaves behind\n\nNo growth endpoint, and none needed: weight and height are recorded on the\nvisit that took them, so the series *is* the visits read in order. The curve\ntherefore cannot disagree with the appointment list -- they are the same rows.\n\nThree rules, each with a test:\n\n- Only COMPLETED, same admission rule as latestMeasuredVisit and for the same\n  reason: the API will hold a measurement on a cancelled visit, but a cancelled\n  visit did not weigh anybody.\n- Oldest first. The appointment list sorts the other way, which is right for\n  \"what happened recently\" and backwards for something read left to right.\n- A visit that recorded one measure and not the other contributes a point to\n  one indicator only. Interpolating across the gap would draw a line asserting\n  a height that nobody measured; the two indicators having different point\n  counts on the same page is the truth about how the visits went.\n\nValues stay in grams and millimetres all the way to the label: the conversion\nlives in measurements.ts and nowhere else.\n\n* feat(growth): a chart that draws one measure over age\n\nNo chart library. The entry chunk is 138 kB gzip and the lightest of the usual\ncandidates adds a third of that again for one line and two axes; this is a\npolyline, four gridlines and some absolutely-positioned text.\n\nThe geometry is SVG and every word is HTML. `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` lets\nthe plot stretch to the card''s width with no arithmetic and\n`vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` keeps the line 2px through the stretch --\nbut text inside that viewBox would be squashed horizontally and would scale with\nthe container instead of with the reader''s font size. HTML labels positioned by\npercentage answer to browser zoom like the rest of the page (WCAG 1.4.4).\n\nTwo measures, two charts, never two axes: a second y scale makes the crossing\npoint of weight and height look like a fact when it is an artefact of where the\nscales were pinned.\n\nThe axis arithmetic is `niceScale`, tested without a browser: 5.4-15.8 becomes\n5/10/15/20 rather than four equal parts at 8.0, 10.6, 13.2. A single\nmeasurement -- min equal to max -- opens a range around the value instead of\ndividing by zero and printing the same number on every tick.\n\nColours are emerald-700/violet-500, and emerald-600/violet-400 in dark. Not\npicked: run through the palette validator against each surface, where the app''s\ndark `--primary` (#34d399) sits outside the dark lightness band and emerald-600\ndoes not.\n\nThree gates in the story, each confirmed able to fail:\n\n- The points are drawn in order and spread. **Not** \"every dot is inside the\n  plot\" -- `positionIn` clamps, so a scale that does not contain its data piles\n  points on the edge and a bounds check stays green. Measured: shrinking the age\n  scale to a third passed the bounds version and fails this one.\n- A dot is >= 24px to hit (WCAG 2.5.8), which is the span around the 8px mark.\n- The line is painted. `stroke-emerald-750` -- a class Tailwind never emits --\n  leaves `stroke: none`, an invisible curve and no error anywhere.\n\n* feat(growth): a page for how the child is growing\n\n`/crescimento`, the seventh section of the menu, and the first thing the side\nmenu made room for: the label needs ~85px, which the bottom bar never had.\n\nIt reads the appointments and owns no endpoint. Weight and height are recorded\non the visit that took them, so the series *is* the visits -- and the curve can\nnever disagree with the appointment list, because they are the same rows.\nReaching into another feature''s `api/` is the sanctioned shape for exactly this\ncase, and the alternative is a second copy that can drift from the first.\n\nOne section per child, not one chart with a line each. Two children are two\ndifferent ages at the same instant: a shared axis either compares them on the\nsame *date*, which says nothing about growth, or stacks curves whose points\nnever line up. The chips filter which sections show -- the same local filter the\nother aggregate pages use, no global \"selected child\".\n\nUnder each pair of charts, the same numbers as a table. It is not a nicety: it\nis what makes the figures readable to someone who cannot see them, which is what\nlets each chart be a single `role=\"img\"` with no focusable dots, and it is the\nonly place the values are exact -- a curve says \"it went up\", a row says 7,9 kg\non 28/07. A visit that recorded one measure and not the other shows an em dash,\nnever a blank: the blank reads as data lost, the dash as a visit that did not\nmeasure.\n\nThree states that are not the happy one, each with a test: a single measurement\nsays so (\"uma medida sozinha ainda não desenha uma curva\" -- a lone dot on a\nfull axis otherwise looks like the rest failed to load); no measurement at all\nsends the person to /appointments, where measurements are entered, instead of\nleaving a dead end; and a cancelled visit that kept a measurement is not counted.\n\nCosts 2.7 kB gzip on the entry chunk (152.970 -> 155.725 bytes, measured), which\nis the icon, the menu row and the copy in three locales. The page itself is a\nlazy 9.7 kB chunk, like every other route.\n\nTwo stale comments in router.tsx went with it: both argued from the bottom bar''s\nwidth arithmetic, which no longer exists. The conclusion about /plano-de-saude\nsurvives, for a reason that is now about what a section is for rather than about\n320px.\n\nlint, tsc -b, 214 unit tests, 93 stories and a full build green.\n\n* fix(growth): an age axis in whole months, and the visit''s own date\n\nBoth found by looking at a seeded page, which is why the capture script exists;\nneither fails a test that was written before them.\n\nA newborn''s only measurement sits at age ~0, so the age axis had nothing to\ndivide and padded itself in both directions: it printed **-1 month**. It now\ntakes a floor of one month on the span and a whole-month step -- \"0,25 meses\" is\na week, said the wrong way. Weight and height pass no minimum and keep their\ndecimals, which a test pins.\n\nThe table read \"07/09/2025\" beside \"1 mês\" for a visit two months after birth.\n`scheduledAt.slice(0, 10)` is the **UTC** date while the age is counted from the\nlocal one, and for a visit late in the day those are different days. Both the\nrow and the tooltip now use `splitScheduledAt`, the same local date the\nappointment card has always shown.\n\nAnd the reason a test could have missed it: **the suite''s timezone was whatever\nthe machine had** -- UTC-3 here, UTC on the runner. At offset zero neither this\nbug nor the `new Date(''2026-01-15'')` one from earlier today is reproducible at\nall, so the regression tests would have been green decoration in CI. `TZ` is now\npinned to America/Sao_Paulo, which is where this app''s users are. Confirmed by\nrestoring the UTC slice: the new test fails, and it fails on the runner too.\n\n218 tests green.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqnhOTCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAIC4QAJZCJ6CZq0RGcWfgNQ9Veqjx\nL8f6cnUAxqxsQB8BEaD5iWtdl/5NmDchsCxW+PHoSAzeyGfFTE3sFqf3GE5tcCjJ\nxmiUwPDBLurpSCWXWlSw+6jkIhvEaNO111cNuLo4zuv3ZPtYmnHlCdIjUSYLHwq9\n4ZjX4qh3QiPYIVeK7i7+fhAcvqmWvFUQv9E0FVQd2YNmS/lgrIXMeqZa5B1mqUZi\ni6PnhyIVJwabxFpSYbcV7CSDQnI2xR2bMGjPvQS5jEyyrdzKqfn2XO7AEfGqB4fg\nvxYH6JTWdlWFeBE1PfZ5GAra0H6/BE4iCwIwHOm7UYCWI08H2MFROzzsm2ohM8/P\nfv49BOcGwYMAhkEDoJXllcRNkfLrGmIAXHMy5ph+P8cY7FOYZA+3ayjHTpiUD6r9\nPrj3SRomPgGGVQJ3XpDbkWDgd9MWnUWTeHToXNvqvSb9Mb5zACpGnfOxFs8QF3no\nH3cRvU7Z/PLea7l9kTHIEwL1+55JgqQLMTco02BK1kawN/Hm2XiE6+53uZD27L1+\n7nG6bh+Z3WoCmzstTsdPYx33XGj3amRc4nBmAWDKA8AKf1UFSJxRB8Zg2moIy5jp\nIAsbCNObP/mAyiJ/iIl+TEKV6Bl0CJbRR4TkTAY8l26luhr43Khq0hh7ebktTx6H\nni0lJ1sVCEnZ+yDs4fho\n=V21+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T01:29:56Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKDBhNmZkZWI2MzZjZWE1YWYwNzAwZTRlZjE5Y2I0M2E3YmU2MjEyYzc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093","sha":"1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093"}],"sha":"0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/0a6fdeb636cea5af0700e4ef19cb43a7be6212c7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-07T00:43:42Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-07T00:43:42Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(nav): a side menu, and the labels that only fit in one (#85)\n\n* feat(nav): a side menu component, measured before it is wired\n\nThe bottom bar''s arithmetic ran out at seven items: each target is\n(width - padding) / items, so 320px left 43.4px and the 44px floor of WCAG\n2.5.5 was only bought back by halving the bar''s own padding. An eighth item\nis 39px at zero padding. The label budget ran out first -- \"Profissionais\"\nneeds ~90px inside a 45px target, which is why that tab has read \"Equipe\".\n\nA vertical list divides nothing horizontally: every row is the width of the\ncolumn, and the only shared budget is height, where a section costs 44px of\nsomething that scrolls. AppSidebar takes its whole tree as props and holds no\nstate, so a story can mount what the shell builds from three stores, a\nmutation and two queries.\n\nTwo gates, both confirmed able to fail before being trusted:\n\n- 44px on every row and on the two account buttons. Shrinking the row to\n  min-h-8/py-1 reports `expected 32 to be greater than or equal to 44`.\n- every label shown whole, read as overflow on the label span. At 272px --\n  the drawer width on a phone, narrower than the desktop column --\n  \"Inicio\" measures 35/35; at 110px the same label measures 35/29 and fails.\n\nThe second gate needed `min-w-0` to exist at all. Without it the flex default\n`min-width: auto` lets the text impose its own width: at 110px nothing\nwrapped, nothing overflowed its own box, and the check passed on a sidebar\nwhose labels were spilling out of it. An equal-height check would have been\nworse -- these labels are single words, and a word too long for its box does\nnot wrap, so no row ever grows.\n\nNothing renders this yet; the shell still ships the bottom bar.\n\n* feat(nav): the shell navigates from a side menu\n\nThe app carried two lists of the same routes: a pill bar in the desktop\nheader and a fixed bottom bar on phones, each with its own item component and\nits own arithmetic. This replaces both with one AppSidebar -- a column from\n`lg` up, the contents of a drawer below it.\n\nWhat that buys, beyond one list instead of two: the labels. In the bottom bar\na label lived inside a target of (width - padding) / items, so at seven items\nit had ~45px at 320px and truncation was routine. A row in a column is as\nwide as the column, and \"Profissionais\" fits at the drawer''s 272px with room\nto spare.\n\nNotifications leaves the section list and becomes the bell in the top bar,\nwhere the unread count is readable from every page instead of only from the\npage it counts. Two links to one route on screen at once would be a second\nthing to keep in sync and an ambiguous target for anyone navigating by name.\nThe count is in the accessible name, not only in the red circle, and the\ncircle itself is aria-hidden so it is not announced as a bare number.\n\nThe top bar is now: menu button (phones), brand or section name, theme,\nbell, account. The account avatar is the initial on a circle -- User is\n{id, email, name, createdAt} in the API, with no photo to show, and the\ninitial is the part of an avatar a top bar actually needs. Add-a-child and\nsign-out move into the menu, next to the account they belong to.\n\nThe drawer is a Dialog rather than a hand-rolled panel: focus trap, Escape,\nscroll lock, click-outside and this repo''s focus-restore fix for dialogs\nopened from state all come with it. `h-dvh` on both the column and the\ndrawer -- with `100vh` the account footer sits under a phone''s URL bar.\n\nlint, tsc -b, 191 unit tests and 90 stories green.\n\n* feat(nav): the sections get their real names back\n\nThree labels were abbreviations of the page they open, and each abbreviation\nwas bought by the same constraint: a bottom-bar target of ~45px at 320px.\n\n  Equipe    -> Profissionais   (the page has always been \"Profissionais\")\n  Avisos    -> Notificações\n  Remédios  -> Medicamentos\n\nNothing divides a column, so the constraint is gone and with it the reason.\nA menu row and the heading it leads to now read the same word in all three\nlocales, which is one less thing for someone to have to learn about this app.\n\n\"Marcos\" stays as it is: the page is \"Marcos de Desenvolvimento\" and the\nshort form is the same word, not a different one.\n\nThe story''s fit gate covers the new labels at 272px -- \"Profissionais\" and\n\"Medicamentos\", the two longest the app ships.\n\nThe a11y sweep clicks navigation by name, so it moves with them; the bell is\nmatched by prefix rather than exactly, since its accessible name grows the\nunread count when there is one. That suite is not in CI -- it needs a\ncheckout of cygnus-api plus Postgres and Redis -- so this is the only thing\nthat would have caught the break.\n\n* fix(nav): the section name in the top bar is not a second h1\n\nEvery route opens with its own h1. The shell had added another one above it,\nso each page carried two, and the outer one is the weaker of the pair -- a\nbare section name, repeated from the menu row highlighted a few centimetres\nto its left.\n\nFound by the E2E, not by review: `getByRole(''heading'', { level: 1 })` stopped\nresolving to a single element and add-baby.spec failed with both headings\nlisted. Neither the unit suite nor axe says anything about two h1s, and the\nE2E does not run in CI -- it needed the local stack to appear at all.\n\n* test(e2e): scan the menu drawer on a phone\n\nPlaywright runs at Desktop Chrome''s width, where the menu is the fixed column\nand the drawer never mounts. Nothing covered it — and below `lg` it is the\nentire navigation, so the untested half is the half a phone uses.\n\nThe spec opens it at 390x844, scans with axe (WCAG 2 A/AA), then navigates\nfrom inside it and asserts it shut behind the tap: a menu that covers the page\nand stays open after a tap leaves the destination unreachable.\n\n8 specs green against the local stack (api + Postgres + Redis + the rebuilt\nweb container). Still not in CI, for the reason it never was: it needs a\ncheckout of cygnus-api.\n\n* style(nav): the section name in the top bar is chrome, not a title\n\nSet in the display face at text-xl it read as a page title, and on\n/profissionais that made the same word twice in the same weight, a few pixels\napart -- the shell''s label and the page''s own heading. Five of the seven\nsections have a heading that starts with the section''s name.\n\nSmall, muted and secondary is also what the reference does: \"Vacinas\" over the\nchild and the date in the top bar, above a much larger \"Calendário de Vacinas\"\non the page.\n\nScreenshots re-taken from the rebuilt container; 8 E2E specs still green.","tree":{"sha":"4f97abebe8a826c90f7a556f9e557f3edefc29f6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/4f97abebe8a826c90f7a556f9e557f3edefc29f6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093","verification":{"payload":"tree 4f97abebe8a826c90f7a556f9e557f3edefc29f6\nparent b25a447c58488994aac312de4e698d5eb9b2b378\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788741822 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788741822 -0300\n\nfeat(nav): a side menu, and the labels that only fit in one (#85)\n\n* feat(nav): a side menu component, measured before it is wired\n\nThe bottom bar''s arithmetic ran out at seven items: each target is\n(width - padding) / items, so 320px left 43.4px and the 44px floor of WCAG\n2.5.5 was only bought back by halving the bar''s own padding. An eighth item\nis 39px at zero padding. The label budget ran out first -- \"Profissionais\"\nneeds ~90px inside a 45px target, which is why that tab has read \"Equipe\".\n\nA vertical list divides nothing horizontally: every row is the width of the\ncolumn, and the only shared budget is height, where a section costs 44px of\nsomething that scrolls. AppSidebar takes its whole tree as props and holds no\nstate, so a story can mount what the shell builds from three stores, a\nmutation and two queries.\n\nTwo gates, both confirmed able to fail before being trusted:\n\n- 44px on every row and on the two account buttons. Shrinking the row to\n  min-h-8/py-1 reports `expected 32 to be greater than or equal to 44`.\n- every label shown whole, read as overflow on the label span. At 272px --\n  the drawer width on a phone, narrower than the desktop column --\n  \"Inicio\" measures 35/35; at 110px the same label measures 35/29 and fails.\n\nThe second gate needed `min-w-0` to exist at all. Without it the flex default\n`min-width: auto` lets the text impose its own width: at 110px nothing\nwrapped, nothing overflowed its own box, and the check passed on a sidebar\nwhose labels were spilling out of it. An equal-height check would have been\nworse -- these labels are single words, and a word too long for its box does\nnot wrap, so no row ever grows.\n\nNothing renders this yet; the shell still ships the bottom bar.\n\n* feat(nav): the shell navigates from a side menu\n\nThe app carried two lists of the same routes: a pill bar in the desktop\nheader and a fixed bottom bar on phones, each with its own item component and\nits own arithmetic. This replaces both with one AppSidebar -- a column from\n`lg` up, the contents of a drawer below it.\n\nWhat that buys, beyond one list instead of two: the labels. In the bottom bar\na label lived inside a target of (width - padding) / items, so at seven items\nit had ~45px at 320px and truncation was routine. A row in a column is as\nwide as the column, and \"Profissionais\" fits at the drawer''s 272px with room\nto spare.\n\nNotifications leaves the section list and becomes the bell in the top bar,\nwhere the unread count is readable from every page instead of only from the\npage it counts. Two links to one route on screen at once would be a second\nthing to keep in sync and an ambiguous target for anyone navigating by name.\nThe count is in the accessible name, not only in the red circle, and the\ncircle itself is aria-hidden so it is not announced as a bare number.\n\nThe top bar is now: menu button (phones), brand or section name, theme,\nbell, account. The account avatar is the initial on a circle -- User is\n{id, email, name, createdAt} in the API, with no photo to show, and the\ninitial is the part of an avatar a top bar actually needs. Add-a-child and\nsign-out move into the menu, next to the account they belong to.\n\nThe drawer is a Dialog rather than a hand-rolled panel: focus trap, Escape,\nscroll lock, click-outside and this repo''s focus-restore fix for dialogs\nopened from state all come with it. `h-dvh` on both the column and the\ndrawer -- with `100vh` the account footer sits under a phone''s URL bar.\n\nlint, tsc -b, 191 unit tests and 90 stories green.\n\n* feat(nav): the sections get their real names back\n\nThree labels were abbreviations of the page they open, and each abbreviation\nwas bought by the same constraint: a bottom-bar target of ~45px at 320px.\n\n  Equipe    -> Profissionais   (the page has always been \"Profissionais\")\n  Avisos    -> Notificações\n  Remédios  -> Medicamentos\n\nNothing divides a column, so the constraint is gone and with it the reason.\nA menu row and the heading it leads to now read the same word in all three\nlocales, which is one less thing for someone to have to learn about this app.\n\n\"Marcos\" stays as it is: the page is \"Marcos de Desenvolvimento\" and the\nshort form is the same word, not a different one.\n\nThe story''s fit gate covers the new labels at 272px -- \"Profissionais\" and\n\"Medicamentos\", the two longest the app ships.\n\nThe a11y sweep clicks navigation by name, so it moves with them; the bell is\nmatched by prefix rather than exactly, since its accessible name grows the\nunread count when there is one. That suite is not in CI -- it needs a\ncheckout of cygnus-api plus Postgres and Redis -- so this is the only thing\nthat would have caught the break.\n\n* fix(nav): the section name in the top bar is not a second h1\n\nEvery route opens with its own h1. The shell had added another one above it,\nso each page carried two, and the outer one is the weaker of the pair -- a\nbare section name, repeated from the menu row highlighted a few centimetres\nto its left.\n\nFound by the E2E, not by review: `getByRole(''heading'', { level: 1 })` stopped\nresolving to a single element and add-baby.spec failed with both headings\nlisted. Neither the unit suite nor axe says anything about two h1s, and the\nE2E does not run in CI -- it needed the local stack to appear at all.\n\n* test(e2e): scan the menu drawer on a phone\n\nPlaywright runs at Desktop Chrome''s width, where the menu is the fixed column\nand the drawer never mounts. Nothing covered it — and below `lg` it is the\nentire navigation, so the untested half is the half a phone uses.\n\nThe spec opens it at 390x844, scans with axe (WCAG 2 A/AA), then navigates\nfrom inside it and asserts it shut behind the tap: a menu that covers the page\nand stays open after a tap leaves the destination unreachable.\n\n8 specs green against the local stack (api + Postgres + Redis + the rebuilt\nweb container). Still not in CI, for the reason it never was: it needs a\ncheckout of cygnus-api.\n\n* style(nav): the section name in the top bar is chrome, not a title\n\nSet in the display face at text-xl it read as a page title, and on\n/profissionais that made the same word twice in the same weight, a few pixels\napart -- the shell''s label and the page''s own heading. Five of the seven\nsections have a heading that starts with the section''s name.\n\nSmall, muted and secondary is also what the reference does: \"Vacinas\" over the\nchild and the date in the top bar, above a much larger \"Calendário de Vacinas\"\non the page.\n\nScreenshots re-taken from the rebuilt container; 8 E2E specs still green.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqngi+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAaKsQAJ3n3ikYK42l26OZrRAK/u8Y\nfFHcyDZ0BDWDYr23VEUDB7EOMh2R19/suKQN+epVqv8pKO/tcThtCdruefBXPzh0\n5yfhuuYMmQ3u3ypv8vHVKYRO0w3avEYDrKsa8f2x9v1fbXodsc2Lnk5vZS5C/N5l\nATazDP7VwONjuADrVdK+L9Nfpzsd2tUj4yczxzP4EVrSE50JodysSl2K5Y2waXGx\n/NrI51BOBNg47zx2HWg7uXTR7mtx9/qPEfHufUJaINliPyW1eMhEQuLrMekCoKrw\nFtoUlXabdnddI2xLdPd3HVoFkzeVwBf3nsI1iP2WtSokWvLNG9awQpkGRmMRTBQj\nvYo6LnSPJs/WVeNWUOcYmQTiGKuUFcuzPabyuUugj03wP3BT8SeGurEJYuE51+2i\n6B7/xoN7EF3C0vPIkT2kXOggyn9KsiyeMQ97y7uF0clGh+4exGIf83J5DMIo3aIA\nMx7jU6SAyfQWaasZWGb0XVBVnjT6AktAYJb0N3j3HGdJ36mcLlULK8YbJFWXpS0P\nFst0Hn+2SgvZ4kKBa5JqAQaag0vTlRkaFJ2SWdOmREZE36fqEyf8Z4L9l7W2j8Jw\nGsa9MxKc4w6LwrFXDhJu27o3ukOZXg1dc0ji8FmFzziuWgUmflX/fqrG6Tb7811H\n2zApRkqCt/pgjKxpWzl1\n=mqDD\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-07T00:43:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKDE0NThkNWJmMzE4YTA2MzI1NGJiYjdhM2RjZGNlYmNkZTYxZDUwOTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/b25a447c58488994aac312de4e698d5eb9b2b378","sha":"b25a447c58488994aac312de4e698d5eb9b2b378","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/b25a447c58488994aac312de4e698d5eb9b2b378"}],"sha":"1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/1458d5bf318a063254bbb7a3dcdcebcde61d5093"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/b25a447c58488994aac312de4e698d5eb9b2b378/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-06T02:27:23Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-06T02:27:23Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(nav): a tab for the care team, and a bar re-measured to hold it (#84)\n\n* fix(nav): the notifications label the bar could never fit\n\n\"Notificações\" needs 62px of the bar and has never had it: 43px at 320,\n49px at 360, 54px at 390. It is the only label that truncates at every\nwidth the app is measured at, so the icon has been carrying the meaning\nalone since the bar existed.\n\n\"Avisos\" is 36px and fits all three with room left. The screen keeps its\nown title — `notifications.title` is untouched, and this is the bar''s\nlabel, which is the one with a width budget.\n\nMeasured on the container build with the app''s own font: a 10px bold\nInter string in a `truncate` span, compared against the target width the\nflex division leaves it.\n\n* refactor(nav): the bottom bar becomes a component with a test\n\nThe bar''s target size is arithmetic — the items are `flex-1`, so each one\nmeasures `(bar width − padding) / items` — and that arithmetic sat as\nmarkup inside `AppShellLayout`, which nothing can render without a\nsession. It has been checked by hand, against a running container, every\ntime an item was added. The measurement kept being right and kept not\nbeing defended.\n\n`MobileNavBar` is that markup, unchanged, plus a story whose `play`\nasserts the 44px floor (WCAG 2.5.5, AAA) in real Chromium at every\n`npm run test:storybook`. The assertion is written as the formula rather\nthan by resizing the canvas: it reads the padding off the computed style\nand the count off the DOM, so it answers for 320px — the narrowest width\nthe app claims — from a canvas that is not a phone. It asserts the equal\ndivision first, because the formula is only true while it holds.\n\nVerified the gate can fail before trusting it: an eighth item reports\n\"expected 39 to be greater than or equal to 44\", and putting the padding\nback to `p-2` at seven items reports 43.43. Both were tried and reverted.\n\n`md:hidden` moves to the shell, and that is not incidental. On the\ncomponent the bar is `display: none` in the story canvas, which has no\nboxes to measure — the same class that makes it a phone-only bar would\nhave made the test measure nothing and pass. Where it applies belongs to\nthe shell anyway, beside the two headers it already switches at the same\nbreakpoint.\n\n* feat(nav): a tab for the care team, and a bar re-measured to hold it\n\n`/profissionais` has been reachable only from the profile page since it\ngot a page of its own (#83). A professional is looked up as often as an\nappointment is, and one screen away from the profile is not where that\nlookup starts. It becomes the seventh tab; `/plano-de-saude` does not,\nand the asymmetry is the difference between something looked up daily\nand a card number looked up at a clinic desk.\n\nThe seventh item does not fit for free. Targets are `flex-1`, so each is\n`(bar width − padding) / items`, and seven under the bar''s `p-2` measure\n43.4px at 320px — below the 44px floor (WCAG 2.5.5, AAA) that six\ncleared at 50.7px. Two paddings answer for two different things:\n\n- the **bar''s**, halved to `px-1`, decides the target: 44.6 / 50.3 /\n  54.6px at 320 / 360 / 390. Halving rather than removing keeps the\n  active pill off the screen edge.\n- the **item''s**, given up entirely, decides only how much of that\n  target the label may use — it cannot change the target at all. With it\n  at `px-1`, \"Consultas\" and \"Remédios\" truncate at every width; without\n  it, nothing truncates at 360 or 390, which is better than what the\n  six-item bar shipped.\n\nSo the bar carries one more destination and cuts fewer words than\nbefore. 320px still truncates those two, and that is the one width where\nseven items cost something.\n\nMeasured on the container build, signed in, at all three widths — and\nagain as five variants against the built CSS, which is how the item''s\npadding turned out to be the lever that mattered. A `px-0.5` variant\nmeasured identical to no padding at all, because Tailwind never\ngenerated that class: a variant that invents a class silently measures\nsomething else.\n\nUsersIcon, not the stethoscope the page itself uses — the bar would\ncarry the same glyph twice, side by side, and at 320px the icon is what\nstill means something after the label truncates.\n\n* test(e2e): follow the bar to its new label and its new tab\n\nThe a11y sweep walks the app by clicking nav links by name, so renaming\nthe notifications label to \"Avisos\" broke it: `waiting for\ngetByRole(''link'', { name: ''Notificações'' })`, then a timeout. It failed\non the first local run after the rename and would not have failed in CI,\nwhich does not run this suite — the label was three commits old by then.\n\nThe sweep also now follows the new tab. `/profissionais` is a main page\nof the app as of this branch, and a page nothing scans is a page whose\nviolations nobody finds; it reports clean, empty state included.\n\nRun against the local stack: 7 passed.","tree":{"sha":"ed0be73d22cd131a3463eec6c9e2f9c7ada34138","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/ed0be73d22cd131a3463eec6c9e2f9c7ada34138"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/b25a447c58488994aac312de4e698d5eb9b2b378","verification":{"payload":"tree ed0be73d22cd131a3463eec6c9e2f9c7ada34138\nparent 06