rs.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/4715065ca45aa73fbe428eb4a5d29e9c69ebc5a0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-03T21:38:49Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-03T21:38:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(vaccines): give the vaccines page a progress card, and fix its denominator (#70)\n\nThe page had the counts in a subtitle — \"42 tomadas · 0 atrasadas · 52\npendentes\" — and no way to read them at a glance. This adds the progress card\nthe LoginAndDashboardDesign reference leads its vaccines screen with: a bar, the\npercentage, and a legend of the three states with a coloured dot each.\n\nOne thing was added in the adaptation: **the card states its own denominator.**\nThe page subtitle counts the whole calendar and the bar counts only doses that\ncan be completed, so a conditional dose that was actually given lands in one\nnumber and not the other — 42 against 40 on the calendar this was built against.\nWithout the \"de 92\", the page shows two different values for the same word\ninches apart, and that reads as a bug rather than as two answers to two\nquestions.\n\nWriting the legend is what surfaced a real defect in the calculation this\ninherited. Filtering by `recommendationKind === ''ROUTINE''` is not enough,\nbecause ROUTINE and GUIDANCE are not mutually exclusive. Measured against a real\nthree-child calendar rather than assumed:\n\n    ROUTINE / APPLIED        40      CONDITIONAL / APPLIED     2\n    ROUTINE / PENDING        52      CONDITIONAL / GUIDANCE    4\n    ROUTINE / GUIDANCE        1      RECURRING  / GUIDANCE     3\n\nThat single ROUTINE/GUIDANCE — \"Febre amarela, situação excepcional\" — sat in\nthe denominator and could never complete, so the bar could not reach 100% by\nconstruction and the legend added up to 92 under a total of 93. A legend that\ndoes not sum to its own total reads as a broken app even when every number in\nit is right.\n\nSo the universe is now routine **and** not guidance. This does change what the\ndashboard shows — the widget it was extracted from had the same bug, quietly,\nwith no legend to expose it. Six unit tests pin the rule, including the\nROUTINE/GUIDANCE case, the divide-by-zero that renders as \"NaN%\" without failing\ntype or lint, and the rounding.\n\nSmaller calls worth stating:\n\n- The bar is `aria-hidden` and the state is carried by text. The percentage is\n  already written beside it, so a `progressbar` role would have a screen reader\n  announce the same number twice, and nothing here is interactive or\n  self-updating — it is a picture of a value the text already gives.\n- The card renders only inside the branch that already proved it has data. A bar\n  at 0% over a calendar that failed to load asserts that nothing was taken,\n  which is the same mistake the subtitle above already avoids by printing no\n  counts on error.\n- Each value sits in its own element instead of two bare text nodes either side\n  of a \"·\". As siblings, \"40 de 92\" was the text of no element at all — the\n  parent read \"40 de 92 · 43%\" — which neither a test nor a screen reader can\n  treat as a unit. The story caught it.\n- The three legend keys are written out rather than assembled as\n  `` t(`…legend.${key}`) ``: audit-locale-parity.mjs reads `t(''…'')` statically,\n  and an assembled key lands in its \"check by eye\" list, which is where a\n  missing translation gets through.\n\nVerified: lint, tsc, 152 unit tests (6 new), 89 story tests (6 new stories,\nincluding the empty, complete and no-routine-doses states), full build.\naudit-locale-parity.mjs clean on all three counts. audit-surface.mjs on\n/vaccines 6/6 with no failures. pages-a11y.mjs 12/12 in both themes. Looked at\nwith three children at 1440px and 360px, light and dark.","tree":{"sha":"b650e5845dc43f8a62b86d9d5026e40391fd5ec2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/b650e5845dc43f8a62b86d9d5026e40391fd5ec2"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/4715065ca45aa73fbe428eb4a5d29e9c69ebc5a0","verification":{"payload":"tree b650e5845dc43f8a62b86d9d5026e40391fd5ec2\nparent 644b20fcb73fa94b340ed90e9d1f0fd9a9ffd415\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788471529 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788471529 -0300\n\nfeat(vaccines): give the vaccines page a progress card, and fix its denominator (#70)\n\nThe page had the counts in a subtitle — \"42 tomadas · 0 atrasadas · 52\npendentes\" — and no way to read them at a glance. This adds the progress card\nthe LoginAndDashboardDesign reference leads its vaccines screen with: a bar, the\npercentage, and a legend of the three states with a coloured dot each.\n\nOne thing was added in the adaptation: **the card states its own denominator.**\nThe page subtitle counts the whole calendar and the bar counts only doses that\ncan be completed, so a conditional dose that was actually given lands in one\nnumber and not the other — 42 against 40 on the calendar this was built against.\nWithout the \"de 92\", the page shows two different values for the same word\ninches apart, and that reads as a bug rather than as two answers to two\nquestions.\n\nWriting the legend is what surfaced a real defect in the calculation this\ninherited. Filtering by `recommendationKind === ''ROUTINE''` is not enough,\nbecause ROUTINE and GUIDANCE are not mutually exclusive. Measured against a real\nthree-child calendar rather than assumed:\n\n    ROUTINE / APPLIED        40      CONDITIONAL / APPLIED     2\n    ROUTINE / PENDING        52      CONDITIONAL / GUIDANCE    4\n    ROUTINE / GUIDANCE        1      RECURRING  / GUIDANCE     3\n\nThat single ROUTINE/GUIDANCE — \"Febre amarela, situação excepcional\" — sat in\nthe denominator and could never complete, so the bar could not reach 100% by\nconstruction and the legend added up to 92 under a total of 93. A legend that\ndoes not sum to its own total reads as a broken app even when every number in\nit is right.\n\nSo the universe is now routine **and** not guidance. This does change what the\ndashboard shows — the widget it was extracted from had the same bug, quietly,\nwith no legend to expose it. Six unit tests pin the rule, including the\nROUTINE/GUIDANCE case, the divide-by-zero that renders as \"NaN%\" without failing\ntype or lint, and the rounding.\n\nSmaller calls worth stating:\n\n- The bar is `aria-hidden` and the state is carried by text. The percentage is\n  already written beside it, so a `progressbar` role would have a screen reader\n  announce the same number twice, and nothing here is interactive or\n  self-updating — it is a picture of a value the text already gives.\n- The card renders only inside the branch that already proved it has data. A bar\n  at 0% over a calendar that failed to load asserts that nothing was taken,\n  which is the same mistake the subtitle above already avoids by printing no\n  counts on error.\n- Each value sits in its own element instead of two bare text nodes either side\n  of a \"·\". As siblings, \"40 de 92\" was the text of no element at all — the\n  parent read \"40 de 92 · 43%\" — which neither a test nor a screen reader can\n  treat as a unit. The story caught it.\n- The three legend keys are written out rather than assembled as\n  `` t(`…legend.${key}`) ``: audit-locale-parity.mjs reads `t(''…'')` statically,\n  and an assembled key lands in its \"check by eye\" list, which is where a\n  missing translation gets through.\n\nVerified: lint, tsc, 152 unit tests (6 new), 89 story tests (6 new stories,\nincluding the empty, complete and no-routine-doses states), full build.\naudit-locale-parity.mjs clean on all three counts. audit-surface.mjs on\n/vaccines 6/6 with no failures. pages-a11y.mjs 12/12 in both themes. Looked at\nwith three children at 1440px and 360px, light and dark.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmejpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmo4QABdIKB6h9C2wB8hmcnzrJUaF\nHfLpIgJ8NWKIKzYrAIqtp4gMAdvo/ZXo0nIcWfATtXfXpETGDSPvsYraIuJKqN10\nFagS2bV/XhwqlbRzdmujJESO2WtYJGU1Fx6Q7nV5KcDf9gxGBQPcrIuAjFsVaWq8\nzN6Ys7OTrNrAcqtRDil6QuEEF0GtIM0XWES3AQT9z/H4SpegKufk5DsWLMCLWW3r\nUk0jc+leeVBQB/I3GoswZkZ4K5vF8NULbhOSL6j6rcLCembA+YNxULKGtK/caZwe\nBnqmuJliW5PmyVDtOGEDSMP6DkMCM5UtqrWCz0vOjKTMUpwd1bBCpmbYSWdiEppn\nA4g/mV3LRdycbLfG2QGI+JzAh0soq4wtss1at9uTeEW9GUDUVLrjqBIG1Az01jZt\nNfpfyQfrWFtkHiPn8lAIYCT6hXrbbqWBXiIYW8E9fG+SroTRK/WTaaQ9KOamrRb9\nAKlUyc28c3b0Xf4G2EjfhFryqPUNIbE3+Z+/jZvTU+Bl0+q3BtZ8toC/3fZ8R5/O\nsU3HhdHTaf2cB1Bmna5ZiuHjdcINbCj3SlA/Ol9R1giqYGofgdvjkyXzqFnqhf9B\nGZBYYoAbPG//kf69YZX/qL3NYbLQgMN+7IfEOqMRLO8fHZ/YZIiJbzKyxxC/urlZ\nYSEYK+BlyNNk8KVVutkE\n=jOJ+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-03T21:38:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/4715065ca45aa73fbe428eb4a5d29e9c69ebc5a0","node_id":"C_kwDOTkG-FdoAKDQ3MTUwNjVjYTQ1YWE3M2ZiZTQyOGViNGE1ZDI5ZTljNjllYmM1YTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commit/644b20fcb73fa94b340ed90e9d1f0fd9a9ffd415","sha":"644b20fcb73fa94b340ed90e9d1f0fd9a9ffd415","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/644b20fcb73fa94b340ed90e9d1f0fd9a9ffd415"}],"sha":"4715065ca45aa73fbe428eb4a5d29e9c69ebc5a0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/4715065ca45aa73fbe428eb4a5d29e9c69ebc5a0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/30626822?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelcsantana","id":30626822,"login":"samuelcsantana","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMwNjI2ODIy","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelcsantana","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/commits/644b20fcb73fa94b340ed90e9d1f0fd9a9ffd415/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-03T21:36:28Z","email":"samuel.ssa89@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Santana"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-03T21:36:28Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(babies): open the dashboard with the children, and show their health data (#69)\n\nThe dashboard led with a greeting and a row of numbers; the children were a row\nof small chips below them. Now each child gets a card at the top — avatar, name,\nage, blood type, allergies and the vaccine status chip the strip used to carry.\n\nThe scale is the visible part. The substance is this: **`bloodType` and\n`allergies` were collected at sign-up and rendered nowhere.** Grep them and the\nonly hits outside the schema are the form fields themselves. A parent typed\ntheir child''s allergy and the app never showed it again — in an app about a\nchild''s health, that was the one field someone needs to find fast, and it was\nwrite-only.\n\nSo the card asserts both sides. With an allergy recorded it names it; with none\nit says there is no *record* — not that there is no allergy. Same distinction\n`vaccineStatusKnown` already made for vaccines, for the same reason: silence\nhere reads as \"all clear\", and nobody checked.\n\nAdapted from the LoginAndDashboardDesign reference, and it needed adapting.\nThat prototype has one active child selected in a sidebar, and this app\naggregates the whole household by design. One hero per child, stacked, is\nSamuel''s call (03/09/2026) made knowing the cost: with six children the row\npushes the vaccines, appointments and milestones cards well below the fold. Two\ncolumns from `lg` up halve that, and were needed anyway — the reference fills a\nfull-width hero with weight, height, paediatrician and health plan, none of\nwhich exist in our model, so at 1440px a full-width card was mostly empty.\n\nColour is measured, not sampled from the reference, whose own hero fails: it\nwrites the panel copy in white at 60% and 45% opacity, 2.42:1 and 1.90:1. Every\nvalue here was measured against the *lighter* gradient stop, which is the worst\ncase across the card, by docs/verification/hero-contrast.mjs:\n\n- white name 5.48, emerald-50 meta 5.21, amber-50 allergies 5.01\n- chips are opaque so their text measures against the chip: rose-700 on rose-50\n  5.67, emerald-700 on emerald-50 5.21, ink on slate-100 14.41; as fills over\n  the gradient they sit between 4.95 and 5.21, past the 3:1 graphical floor\n\nTwo obvious approaches failed and the script keeps them as regression cases:\n`text-white/70` and `/80` give 3.54 and 4.11, and a `white/15` chip gives 4.13.\nNothing on this card uses alpha on text. The gradient also stops at emerald-700\nrather than emerald-600 because at 600 not even pure white passes (3.77).\n\n**No gate covers any of that** — axe reads the nearest opaque ancestor and does\nnot evaluate text over a gradient, so pages-a11y.mjs passes this card without\nhaving looked at it. Hence the script, and hence the ratios cited at each call\nsite.\n\nFamilyStrip stays, for /vaccines only, and its header now says why the two are\nnot a duplication to collapse: the hero says who the child is and carries the\nhealth fields; the strip says which child is which above a long list that is\nthat page''s actual content. Heroes there would push the list below the fold and\nrepeat the vaccine chip on the one screen where vaccine status is the subject.\nThe shared i18n keys were renamed `familyStrip*` -> `vaccineStatus*` for the\nsame reason — they belong to the information, not to either component.\n\nThe edit button is a real 44x44 here rather than 28x28 with an `-inset-2.5`\npseudo-element over it. Both give the same clickable area, but audit-surface.mjs\nmeasures the button''s own rect and only sees the first, so the strip shows up as\nthree sub-44px warnings that are not true. The card has room; no reason to pay\nthe warning.\n\nVerified: lint, tsc, 154 unit tests (8 new, covering the three vaccine-status\ncases inherited from the strip plus the allergy and blood-type states), 83 story\ntests, full build. audit-locale-parity.mjs clean on all three counts after the\nrename. audit-surface.mjs on /dashboard 6/6 with no failures, and the three\n\"Editar perfil de X\" warnings gone. pages-a11y.mjs 12/12 in both themes. Looked\nat with three children at 1440px and 360px, light and dark.","tree":{"sha":"5c67fe3dffd5503992b4da7725c23eded39d2eb9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/trees/5c67fe3dffd5503992b4da7725c23eded39d2eb9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/samuelcsantana/cygnus/git/commits/644b20fcb73fa94b340ed90e9d1f0fd9a9ffd415","verification":{"payload":"tree 5c67fe3dffd5503992b4da7725c23eded39d2eb9\nparent 3d24b9a7d6c1a3fe4ff2d44ab21fd099f5e01c38\nauthor Samuel Santana <samuel.ssa89@gmail.com> 1788471388 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788471388 -0300\n\nfeat(babies): open the dashboard with the children, and show their health data (#69)\n\nThe dashboard led with a greeting and a row of numbers; the children were a row\nof small chips below them. Now each child gets a card at the top — avatar, name,\nage, blood type, allergies and the vaccine status chip the strip used to carry.\n\nThe scale is the visible part. The substance is this: **`bloodType` and\n`allergies` were collected at sign-up and rendered nowhere.** Grep them and the\nonly hits outside the schema are the form fields themselves. A parent typed\ntheir child''s allergy and the app never showed it again — in an app about a\nchild''s health, that was the one field someone needs to find fast, and it was\nwrite-only.\n\nSo the card asserts both sides. With an allergy recorded it names it; with none\nit says there is no *record* — not that there is no allergy. Same distinction\n`vaccineStatusKnown` already made for vaccines, for the same reason: silence\nhere reads as \"all clear\", and nobody checked.\n\nAdapted from the LoginAndDashboardDesign reference, and it needed adapting.\nThat prototype has one active child selected in a sidebar, and this app\naggregates the whole household by design. One hero per child, stacked, is\nSamuel''s call (03/09/2026) made knowing the cost: with six children the row\npushes the vaccines, appointments and milestones cards well below the fold. Two\ncolumns from `lg` up halve that, and were needed anyway — the reference fills a\nfull-width hero with weight, height, paediatrician and health plan, none of\nwhich exist in our model, so at 1440px a full-width card was mostly empty.\n\nColour is measured, not sampled from the reference, whose own hero fails: it\nwrites the panel copy in white at 60% and 45% opacity, 2.42:1 and 1.90:1. Every\nvalue here was measured against the *lighter* gradient stop, which is the worst\ncase across the card, by docs/verification/hero-contrast.mjs:\n\n- white name 5.48, emerald-50 meta 5.21, amber-50 allergies 5.01\n- chips are opaque so their text measures against the chip: rose-700 on rose-50\n  5.67, emerald-700 on emerald-50 5.21, ink on slate-100 14.41; as fills over\n  the gradient they sit between 4.95 and 5.21, past the 3:1 graphical floor\n\nTwo obvious approaches failed and the script keeps them as regression cases:\n`text-white/70` and `/80` give 3.54 and 4.11, and a `white/15` chip gives 4.13.\nNothing on this card uses alpha on text. The gradient also stops at emerald-700\nrather than emerald-600 because at 600 not even pure white passes (3.77).\n\n**No gate covers any of that** — axe reads the nearest opaque ancestor and does\nnot evaluate text over a gradient, so pages-a11y.mjs passes this card without\nhaving looked at it. Hence the script, and hence the ratios cited at each call\nsite.\n\nFamilyStrip stays, for /vaccines only, and its header now says why the two are\nnot a duplication to collapse: the hero says who the child is and carries the\nhealth fields; the strip says which child is which above a long list that is\nthat page''s actual content. Heroes there would push the list below the fold and\nrepeat the vaccine chip on the one screen where vaccine status is the subject.\nThe shared i18n keys were renamed `familyStrip*` -> `vaccineStatus*` for the\nsame reason — they belong to the information, not to either component.\n\nThe edit button is a real 44x44 here rather than 28x28 with an `-inset-2.5`\npseudo-element over it. Both give the same clickable area, but audit-surface.mjs\nmeasures the button''s own rect and only sees the first, so the strip shows up as\nthree sub-44px warnings that are not true. The card has room; no reason to pay\nthe warning.\n\nVerified: lint, tsc, 154 unit tests (8 new, covering the three vaccine-status\ncases inherited from the strip plus the allergy and blood-type states), 83 story\ntests, full build. audit-locale-parity.mjs clean on all three counts after the\nrename. audit-surface.mjs on /dashboard 6/6 with no failures, and the three\n\"Editar perfil de X\" warnings gone. pages-a11y.mjs 12/12 in both themes. Looked\nat with three children at 1440px and 360px, light and dark.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmehcCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAEjwQAA+J8Y25VAK7gwiTS1/DTOQF\nNkqJuuS/pVAF4Po2li067kdT3vb+d5OHKzMh4Dzn1N8738NjlR6pRwxpd3oRi0Ub\nUUlmJ7IEeoa5vJyWE6ZaB5OVdYxvRPr9Kdk78bjNpzKvJmfqh1SRu7YuMuMIZkOl\nRH9jT4dMZ6as2dqKFbwiw3h1PEOIDJHCSY/+CLRcwruXmabUG5npOqAM62VWpkcG\nILgH59Pgx3yeXb