alse,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/858b6c2aa8f765c592ab7a81ef4cec59eede8727","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKDg1OGI2YzJhYThmNzY1YzU5MmFiN2E4MWVmNGNlYzU5ZWVkZTg3Mjc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/9cccf723c7fb1d872f1a3d40c202da216b7e7a2e","sha":"9cccf723c7fb1d872f1a3d40c202da216b7e7a2e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/9cccf723c7fb1d872f1a3d40c202da216b7e7a2e"},{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","sha":"5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b"}],"sha":"858b6c2aa8f765c592ab7a81ef4cec59eede8727","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/858b6c2aa8f765c592ab7a81ef4cec59eede8727"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/5b8ccd1bf1ef94636ccc4661b77cfcee02093ad8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Wire --backend into migrate and prove the loop\n\nUntil here nothing could ask a RAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT question with real\nchoices through the binary, and nothing could record the answer: the\nCLI never opened a document, `handoff.RouteRow` had nowhere to put an\nendpoint, and `Handoff.backend` was hard-coded null. Every such finding\noffered `retain, blocked` and nothing else, on every app.\n\n`--backend FILE` is that flag. Rails-only and, like `--decisions`, NOT\ngated on `--target`: without a target it still widens what the manifest\npublishes, and the manifest is what an operator reads before writing\nany answers. It is read before `discover`, because `findings.derive`\nand `decisions.parse` both run inside it. There is deliberately no\ndefault location -- a document the operator did not name is a document\nthis run does not have -- and the manifest carries nothing about the\nFILE, only the wider choices, so two operators with one document at two\npaths produce the same bytes. An unparseable file exits 1 naming the\nreason (`InvalidJson`: truncated or hand-edited; `NotOpenApi3`: the\nwrong file entirely; `NoPaths`: generated against an empty data dir)\nrather than degrading to `retain, blocked`, which would look exactly\nlike a document with no matching operation. An unusable `--backend` or\n`--decisions` PATH prints `error: <flag> <path> could not be read` and\nexits 1 too (ruling S3-R4): both ended in `fatal.file`, which panics\nunder a Debug build -- the build every shell e2e drives -- so a typo\narrived as SIGABRT 134 under Zig''s banner. The flag is named because\nthe two fail identically at the OS level.\n\nThe completion rule reopens (ruling A2). S11 accounted a `backend` route\nunconditionally and promised that \"which endpoint\" would ride on its\nown finding in Stage 3. That finding exists, so `statusAccounted` keeps\nthe promise: a `backend` row counts iff bound (`endpoint`) or\nacknowledged (`decision`). This does not restore the over-strictness\n`isUserFacing` exists to avoid -- a POST never reaches the count -- so\nthe one route it bites on is a GET that renders JSON, exactly where\n\"nobody said what this URL becomes\" is a real gap. An app whose `/feed`\nsilently vanished used to report `complete: true`. `orderEndpoint`,\nwritten in Stage 2 against this day so `entryOrder` would not quietly\nbecome a partial order, is now load-bearing and has the test that fails\nwithout it. The handoff gains a `backend` object (`file` is the\nBASENAME: the handoff is committed and no artifact carries a path) and\na real `endpoint` per bound route; `MIGRATION.md`''s `## Handoff`\ngains `backend: <file> (<version>)` / `backend: none` and `endpoints:\nN of the M` -- both unconditional, because the operator wondering why\ntheir findings offered only retain/blocked is precisely the one who\nforgot the flag. `rails-drift.sh` re-anchors its handoff mutation\ninside the `RouteEntry` block, since `RouteRow` now has an `endpoint`\ntoo and the bare anchor matched twice -- the uniqueness assertion doing\nits job -- and its `grep -c` counts gain `|| true`, because under `set\n-e` an absent anchor killed the script before the line that names it.\n\nThe fixture grows the five shapes that make the stage''s one claim\ntrue -- that a Rails app becomes a finished static site BECAUSE its\nmutations were bound: `resource :session` gains `:destroy`; `get\n\"/feed\"` renders JSON (the one route A2 bites on, so `--backend` is\nload-bearing rather than merely widening a list); `before_action\n:require_login, only: [:index]` raises the guard and moves\nRAILS_LAYOUT_DYNAMIC to L3; `password_confirmation` on the sign-up\nform; and `_nav` reads `current_user` in a complementary pair with\n`button_to \"Sign out\", session_path, method: :delete` inside the\nsigned-in half, rendered by both layouts so every page route inherits\nits findings (S21) and run 1 has no migrated route at all. Both\n`controller:` overrides are gone: they were the workaround for #176,\nclosed two chapters ago, so the fixture now exercises the real rule.\nbackend/openapi.json is a REAL `zigbase openapi` document, written by\nthe binary against the two collections this app maps onto, checked in\nbecause CI has no ZigBase; schema.json and the README are the\nregeneration recipe, so the artifact cannot rot silently. The decisions\nfile is re-derived from run 1 and answers the journey `island` with\nartifact `users`, the guard `public`, the feed `listPosts`, and five\nregions `island`; it deliberately does NOT answer the sign-out link,\nwhich the AuthStatus region''s answer already settles.\n\ntests/migrate/rails-presentation.sh proves the loop end to end: run 1\nexits 3 with every new id and its exact choices pinned (the feed\noffered the document''s GET operations and nothing of another verb, the\nunanswered link keeping `GET /` open with no `<a href=\"/session\">` in\nthe nav); run 1b without the flag narrows every answer to retain/blocked\nand the journey message says which flag produces the third; run 2\nexits 0 with `complete: true`, six migrated routes, the four bindings\nnamed operation by operation, the exact 29-path tree, one AuthStatus\nmount for the pair, and the per-route notes pinned as exact strings\nbecause a note that states one fact twice passes a presence check just\nas happily; run 2c drops `--runtime-path` and pins the #179 default; the\ntarget BUILDS and `doctor` reports zero warnings where Stage 2 allowed\none dangling link to `/session/new`; the negatives cover both exit-1\npaths, an artifact naming no auth collection, a wrong-verb operation,\n`custom:/api/feed`, the same answers WITHOUT `--backend` refused, and\nthe superseded sign-out answer accepted; and the guard-only arm drops\nexactly the two `retain` answers the fixture''s own rationale names so\n`public` writes the page and says so. Determinism covers the islands,\nlib/zb.ts and build.sh. tests/migrate/rails.sh pins the `--backend`\ncontract against the repo''s own contract/zigbase.openapi.json, the four\nrejection paths, the `--doctor` exclusion, the control run that must\nstill exit 3 without any answers file, and the whole `## Handoff`\nsection byte for byte; its exact listing is the one assertion that\nwould notice a `lib/zb.ts` written unconditionally.\n\n`--runtime-path`''s help said the Rails half of \"the target has JS to\ninstall\" was the `.spa.tsx` a `spa` decision produced. Every island a\nbackend answer binds emits a package.json too, and neither that nor\nthe ZIGAPAGOS_RUNTIME_DIR fallback was in the help, so an operator had\nno reason to pass the flag on an ordinary answered run and no way to\nlearn the variable their shell already exports would have answered.\nPinned by two greps over `migrate --help`. Closes #187.\n\ndocs/migration/rails-to-zigapagos.md gains section 18, \"The backend\nboundary\" -- what `--backend` reads and the `allAuthMethods` caveat,\nthe three RAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT shapes and the (line, verb, resource)\nid, `custom:/<path>`, what an answered finding produces down to the\nisland bytes, the auth journey and its rules, RAILS_ROUTE_AUTH_GUARD,\nthe exit codes, and the known limitations reproduced against this\nbuild -- and corrects every sentence the stage made false: the advice\nto write `controller:` on every singular `resource` (withdrawn, #176),\n`requires_artifact` \"never true\", `endpoint`/`redirects[].to`/`backend`\n\"always null\", `complete` accounting a `backend` route on its status\nalone, and section 13''s fixture walk-through, now sixteen routes with\nzero doctor warnings. Every quoted id, note, count and listing comes\nfrom a real run. The skill''s step 5 passes `--backend` on EVERY run of\nthe loop and gives the three `zigbase` commands that produce the\ndocument; step 7 says which answers settle a route and which need an\nartifact or the document''s own verb-filtered list; the ZigBase half of\nthe port is handed off explicitly. changelog.d/rails-backend.md records\nthe stage, and rails-convert.md, shipping in the same release, is\ncorrected in place where it said backend endpoints are not produced,\nthat `complete` accepts `backend` alone, and that the two fields are\nalways null -- rather than left to publish beside its own refutation,\nexactly as that fragment did for rails-target.md. The docs registry\ngains the third job so the published card does not still promise two.\n\nKnown gaps carried as issues rather than fixed here: the literal\n`backend` choice on RAILS_REQUEST_TIME_STATE has no converter (#184),\nthe emitted tsconfig makes `tsc` report TS7026 on every island (#185),\nand `--doctor` at a missing directory still exits 134 (#186).\n\nPart of #167: Stage 3 of five. Stages 4 and 5 (interactivity, parity)\nfollow on their own branches, and the last of them closes the issue.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"99303d8775021dff683a54c5b57cabed6a84ee7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/99303d8775021dff683a54c5b57cabed6a84ee7d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/5b8ccd1bf1ef94636ccc4661b77cfcee02093ad8","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/5b8ccd1bf1ef94636ccc4661b77cfcee02093ad8","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKDViOGNjZDFiZjFlZjk0NjM2Y2NjNDY2MWI3N2NmY2VlMDIwOTNhZDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8","sha":"8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8"}],"sha":"5b8ccd1bf1ef94636ccc4661b77cfcee02093ad8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/5b8ccd1bf1ef94636ccc4661b77cfcee02093ad8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Turn answers into form, click and auth islands\n\nThe previous chapter gave the operator a question -- which backend\noperation does this mutation become? -- and nothing read the answer. A\nform answered `createPosts` converted exactly as an unanswered one did:\na `rails:finding` marker around markup that submits nowhere, a route\nthat could never reach `migrated`, and a target with no client code in\nit at all. This is the converter and scaffold half.\n\nA bound region is no longer converted. convert.zig replaces the whole\n`form` block, the mutating `link_to`/`button_to` node, or the\n`current_user` region with one `<island src=... client:load></island>`\nand reports every finding id inside it as ANSWERED rather than open,\nwhich is what lets the route finish. It also absorbs the `errors`\nsummary naming the form''s model -- Rails writes that as a sibling above\nthe form, and the island already renders `ZigbaseError.data` -- which\ncloses one instance of #181 by construction: the `full_messages.each do\n|m|` local inside it never reaches the converter, so it can no longer\nbecome an id-less `rails:unmapped local` no decision could name. A\nbinding is matched by finding id, or by position (`Binding.at`) for the\njourney''s forms, which raise no finding of their own (A5) and share the\njourney''s id -- an id join would let the sign-in binding claim the\nsign-up form. On the way, `request_state`/`errors` branches are paired\nwith their `block_end` the way `control` already was, so `<% if\ncurrent_user %>...<% end %>` is a region and not a marker followed by\nan immediate end with its body converted as though unconditional.\n\nscaffold.zig emits what the island needs: components/forms/<view\nstem>.island.tsx with one control per form-builder helper and a call\nread off the verb and the collection -- `zb.collection(c).create/\nupdate/delete` on a collection operation, `zb.send(verb, path, {body})`\non a consumer route or a `custom:` answer, and for an update or delete\nwith no id field an honest `TODO: this form acts on one record; pass\nits id` rather than a submit that can only fail at runtime; lib/zb.ts\nas `createClient(\"\", { authStore: new LocalAuthStore() })` (A1, what\nthe client actually exports); `@zigbase/client` 0.3.0 in package.json;\none `--island=` per file in build.sh; and `@z/runtime` defaulting to\n`ZIGAPAGOS_RUNTIME_DIR` when no `--runtime-path` was given (#179),\nbecause a package.json is now the ordinary outcome of an answered run\nrather than a `spa` rarity. The redirect after a successful submit is\nthe PAIRED action''s own `redirect_to` (`posts#new` is answered by\n`posts#create`, Rails'' convention) through `resolve.redirectUrl`; the\nsame call fills `Redirect.to`, null since Stage 2, and the paired\nnon-GET route gets the endpoint, since a page route never has one. A\nshared bound partial is written once, keyed on the binding''s identity\nrather than its path, and two view stems that flatten to one island\nname (`a_b/new`, `a/b_new`) get two islands by template ordinal --\nbefore that the second write either aborted on the exclusive-create\nguard or silently shipped `/y` with `/x`''s form.\n\nA mutating link answered becomes a click island: the control''s own\ntext, its `data-confirm`/`data-turbo-confirm` guard, the MUTATING\naction''s redirect, and an endpoint paired onto the route the link names\noutright. Unanswered, it becomes a `rails:finding` region and keeps its\npage open, decided by the same `findings.nodeVerb` that raised the\nfinding. Before this, `button_to \"Sign out\", session_path, method:\n:delete` converted to `<a href=\"/session\">` -- a GET navigation to the\nroute it was meant to DELETE -- on a page the handoff reported\nmigrated with nothing open. A sentinel that still reaches a bound\ncontrol (an older sidecar) is a comment beside the island plus a route\nnote, never silence, and the converter''s own markers -- `form=\"{...}\"`,\nan inert `method=\"get\"` -- are kept out of the templates it writes.\n\n`island` on RAILS_AUTH_JOURNEY with an auth-collection artifact\nscaffolds ONE components/AuthForm.island.tsx for both halves of the\nflow (they differ by a `create` call and a label, which is a `mode`\nprop, so the file''s bytes depend on the journey alone and either view\nmay write it), components/AuthStatus.island.tsx for the `current_user`\nregions -- signed-out branch on the server, flipped after hydration,\nbecause the session lives in the visitor''s browser and reading the\nstore during the first render would make the two disagree -- lib/zb.ts\nwith `authCollection` set exactly when a scaffold reached the target,\nand the three journey endpoints: `authWithPassword`, `logout` and\n`createUsers`. The first two are CollectionService METHOD names, not\noperation ids, because `allAuthMethods` is always false and the\ndocument has none; the third is re-derived by the same `<verb><Base>`\nrule so the trio cannot disagree with itself when the document changes.\nA complementary `if X`/`unless X` pair in one template is ONE mount with\nthe folded half named in the route''s note; two regions of the same\npolarity, two predicates, or the same pair in two templates stay two\nmounts, because a duplicate is a visible blemish while a wrong pairing\nsilently deletes a control. A journey detected only by a password form\nhas no `sessions#new` route and renders no sign-in link rather than one\nthe built site''s own `doctor` would report dangling.\n\nHow answers are applied changed twice under review, and both rulings\nare here. Every answer on a route is applied, strongest first, and an\nacknowledgement stops the walk (ruling S3-R7): a guarded page with a\nbound form used to have the guard''s `public` parsed, validated and then\nsilently dropped, because `pickDecision` returns one answer and the\nform''s id won the tie-break -- three correct answers left both page\nroutes open with nothing left to say. `retain` and `blocked` settle the\nroute rather than anything on it, so carrying on past one would file\nnotes about work that is not happening. And an answer on a finding\ninside a region an island replaced is ACCEPTED and recorded as\nsuperseded, naming both ids (ruling S3-R6) -- the obvious second move\nfrom the handoff''s list, and it used to exit 3 blaming a `--backend`\ndocument that had been passed -- silently where the status-region walk\nalready reports that region as folded or enclosed, so one fact is not\nstated twice in two vocabularies on one line. `public` on the guard\nships the page and names the filter it overrode through the one picker\nthe finding uses (`controllers.authGuardFor`), so the manifest and the\nhandoff cannot name two different filters for one decision; `backend`\non RAILS_REQUEST_TIME_STATE points at #184 instead of promising Stage 3;\nand the journey id rides on every journey route the way a layout''s\nrides on every route under it (ruling S21), which is what lets a\n`retain` on it settle anything.\n\nVerified past the unit tests: targets assembled from the golden bytes\nwent through a real `zigapagos release`, both auth islands bundled and\nhydrated in both auth states, and a scratch app with a `button_to` put\n`custom /api/logout` on `DELETE /logout` in the handoff with that\nroute''s own finding still unanswered, so the endpoint can only have\ncome from the link. Through the binary none of this is reachable yet:\nmigrate.zig still passes no document, so no operation id can be\noffered or accepted; the flag, the handoff fields the endpoint lands\nin, and the fixture that proves the loop are the next chapter.\n\nPart of #167\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"ee8b92b11416e81fcf26bfe9a72ebaabfd1bdeb1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/ee8b92b11416e81fcf26bfe9a72ebaabfd1bdeb1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKDhjNjk5ODUxNGJmNDM4OGU3YTFiMzkwMzE4YzJlMDk0NzY3NjIxZDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9","sha":"ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9"}],"sha":"8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/8c6998514bf4388e7a1b390318c2e094767621d8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Ask which backend operation each mutation becomes\n\nStage 2 could see that a form, a `button_to` or a POST route needed\nsomething the static tree cannot provide, and said so with two words:\n`retain` or `blocked`. Neither migrates anything, so every route\ncarrying one was reachable only by giving up on it. And the route-level\nshapes had no finding at all -- an id-less note in scaffold.zig that no\nMIGRATION.decisions.json line could name -- so `complete` was\nunreachable for the whole app by any answer an operator could give\n(#182 for two of them).\n\n`findings.derive` now takes the ZigBase document `--backend` names, and\na RAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT finding''s `choices` are the operations from\nthat document that could actually serve this submission -- the form''s\nown method against the resource its route names -- rather than a fixed\npair of words. The row covers three shapes with one code: the\nform/form_field region (id unchanged); a `link_to`/`button_to` that\nperforms a mutation, with the verb read from `method:`, Turbo''s\n`data-turbo-method`, rails-ujs'' `data-method` or `button_to`''s POST\ndefault, an explicit `get` on any of them meaning \"not a mutation\", and\nthe choices ranked by the target ROUTE''s controller because Rails''\nmember helper is singular while a collection is plural, so keying on\nthe stem could never put the right collection first (`linkTargetRoute`\nis the one lookup the scaffold reuses to pair the link onto its route);\nand every route that is API traffic by classification or by verb,\nminus the `redirect`-classified ones, which the scaffold settles before\nany verb is read. That last row is the only route-scoped code that does\nnot fold a routes.rb line into one question (ruling S22): `resources\n:posts` declares three verbs on one line and `resources :posts,\n:comments` two resources, and no single operation serves either set, so\nit groups by (line, verb, resource) and carries both in its id --\n`RAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT.config/routes%2Erb.L7.POST.posts`, the resource\nalways emitted so the id has a fixed component count (ruling S3-R2).\n`routeVerbFindingId` is exported so scaffold.zig recomputes the same\nbytes, and the grouping walk sorts on every key it groups by so a split\nrun cannot emit two findings under one id.\n\nFour rows are new:\n\n- RAILS_AUTH_JOURNEY (assumption A5), at most one per app, keyed on the\n  smallest routes.rb line any journey route was declared on. The\n  sign-in and sign-up flow is one decision because the answer to all\n  of it is one ZigBase auth collection; asking each form separately let\n  an operator answer them inconsistently. A route is a journey route\n  when its controller is `sessions`/`registrations` or its own view\n  holds a password form, and journey views -- and, through the render\n  graph Stage 1 already built, the partials they render -- raise no\n  endpoint question of their own. A partial reached from a journey view\n  AND an ordinary one is the journey''s (ruling S3-R3): answered twice\n  it becomes two conflicting bindings for one region, whereas the\n  ordinary page is still covered by the island the journey produces.\n  It is the first finding with `requires_artifact`, because the\n  collection name is a fact about the destination, not something\n  reading the Rails app recovers.\n- RAILS_ROUTE_AUTH_GUARD (A7): a page whose controller -- or a class it\n  inherits from, through `controllers.guardsFor` -- runs a\n  `before_action` whose name contains login, auth, sign or user. A\n  static page cannot enforce it, and shipping it silently public is\n  exactly the \"silently marked complete\" #167 exists to prevent.\n  `public` is a new choice word: ship the page, the ZigBase rules still\n  protect the data. The message names the smallest `(name, line)` among\n  matches rather than the chain-order first, because the order within\n  one controller is whatever order the sidecar''s directory walk emitted\n  and a filter name printed into the manifest has to be the same on\n  every machine.\n- RAILS_CONTENT_PATH_COLLISION and RAILS_ROUTE_PATH_UNSUPPORTED (#182):\n  the two id-less scaffold notes, given ids. Both come from the new\n  `resolve.contentClaims`, which restates scaffold.zig''s route order and\n  exclusions on purpose: which route WINS a content path depends on the\n  order routes are visited in, so the finding and the note have to read\n  one function or they will eventually disagree about who the loser is.\n\n`Finding.choices` becomes owned, both levels. A per-question list built\nfrom a document cannot be a comptime constant, and it is a DEEP copy\nrather than a slice over `backend.choicesFor`''s borrowed elements\nbecause a Finding is handed to manifest.zig, report.zig and\ndecisions.zig with no handle on the document those strings live in;\nborrowing would make three consumers'' correctness depend on a lifetime\nnone of them can check. A test literal pointing at a static list is\nstill fine as long as it never reaches `free`. `isDynamicRoutePath`\nmoves to resolve.zig beside `contentPath`, whose refusal is what\n\"dynamic\" means, so there is no second copy of the loop to drift.\n\n`decisions.parse` gains three rules. A3: `custom:/<path>` answers a\nRAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT finding even though no fixed `choices[]` could\nenumerate it -- the shape is validated instead (absolute, no whitespace\nor quote, since it is interpolated into generated TypeScript), and the\nfinding''s own message says the answer exists. A4: `requires_artifact`\ndemands a path only from a choice that produces something; `retain`\nand `blocked` produce nothing, and the Stage 2 rule was written when no\nfinding set the flag. And an `island` answer on the journey whose\nartifact is not an auth collection the document carries is refused\nwith the list -- otherwise it builds, ships, and fails at the first\nsign-in. Without a document the name is accepted verbatim, since\nrefusing every name would make the finding unanswerable without a flag\nits message promises is optional.\n\n`resolve.redirectUrl` turns the sidecar''s `redirects[]` into a site\nURL: the first entry that resolves wins, and a `dynamic` one is\nskipped rather than ending the search, because an action whose first\nbranch is `redirect_to @post` and whose second is `redirect_to\nroot_path` really does have a target for the second.\n\n`rails.discover` threads all of it: `DecisionsInput.backend`, the\nfilter set, the view each route resolved (the same `resolve.viewFor`\nthe scaffold uses, so the two cannot disagree which template belongs\nto which route), the content claims and the render graph. Nothing yet\nproduces anything from an answer, and the CLI still passes no document,\nso through `migrate` every RAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT keeps offering\nretain/blocked; a bound operation is recorded and the route stays open\non its note until the scaffold learns to read it. Unit-test ids move\nwherever a route table holds a non-GET or backend route, because their\nid now carries the verb and the resource.\n\nPart of #167\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"3c870ccdec2f767d72e95928911839639132611b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/3c870ccdec2f767d72e95928911839639132611b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKGViYmRlMGE3MzZiNjExOGY1ODhkZTgwZjQzYzI0MzIxNDBhOWQzZDk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc","sha":"7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc"}],"sha":"ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/ebbde0a736b6118f588de80f43c2432140a9d3d9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T14:20:52Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Recover what the backend boundary needs from Rails\n\nStage 2 (PR #183) accepts three answers it cannot carry out. `backend`\nis recorded and the route stays `open` with \"deferred to Stage 3\";\nevery non-GET route sits at status `backend` with no endpoint (ruling\nS11 counted it as accounted precisely so completion stayed reachable);\nand a form''s finding offers only retain/blocked, because a form that\ncan be bound to nothing has nothing else to offer. Stage 3 is the\nbackend boundary -- `--backend openapi.json` binds forms, mutating\nlinks and the auth journey to a ZigBase contract -- and this chapter\nrecovers the facts that binding needs, from both sides of it, before\nanything reads them.\n\nFrom the ZigBase side, backend.zig reads the document `zigbase openapi`\nwrites. Two documents years apart must both parse -- the in-repo\ncontract/zigbase.openapi.json (3.0.3, three hand-written consumer\nroutes, an x-zigbase-contract-version, no coverage object) and today''s\n3.1.2 with a coverage object, no contract version and a CRUD block per\ncollection -- so only `openapi` and `paths` are required, and an\nunknown key or x-zigbase-* spelling is ignored rather than reported:\nthe document is ZigBase''s, not the operator''s, and an unknown spelling\nmeans a newer ZigBase, not a mistake to fail on. An auth collection is\nrecognised by its <Base>Create schema carrying both `password` and\n`passwordConfirm`, resolved one hop through $ref. That pair is the only\nobservable marker the document has, and both halves of the conjunction\nare pinned separately because `password` alone is an ordinary field on\na secrets vault, and treating such a collection as an auth collection\nwould offer it as a RAILS_AUTH_JOURNEY artifact. `choicesFor` ranks the\nresource''s own operations first, then every other operation of the\nsame verb, then retain and blocked -- never an operation of a different\nverb. The FailingAllocator sweep earned its place immediately: it\ncaught a loop-scoped errdefer double-freeing an operation `append` had\nalready taken ownership of.\n\nFrom the Rails side, the `controllers` op grows what Stage 2 never\nasked. `only_redirect` said THAT an action redirects and never where,\nwhich is why the handoff''s `redirects[].to` has been null since Stage 2\nand why a form island replacing a Rails mutation would have nowhere to\nsend the browser; and nothing was recovered about `before_action`, so a\npage whose controller runs `require_login` would be converted and\nreported migrated -- shipping silently public exactly the content Rails\nwas gating, the failure #167 calls out by name. So each action carries\n`redirects[]` -- one per `redirect_to` anywhere in the body, in source\norder, as a route-helper stem plus literal args, a literal `path`, or\n`dynamic` (`redirect_back` is dynamic rather than nothing, because an\nEMPTY list says \"this action does not redirect\") -- and the response\ncarries `before_actions[]`, `skip_before_actions[]` and `parents[]`.\n\nThe chain is the point. The commonest Rails guard is one\n`before_action :authenticate_user!` on ApplicationController with every\ncontroller inheriting it; a filter arrives keyed `application` while\nthe route that runs it names `posts`, and matching only the route''s\nown controller found nothing. Filters are therefore reported where\nthey are DECLARED, and controllers.zig''s `guardsFor` walks `class Child\n< Parent`, honouring a `skip_before_action` only at or below the\nfilter''s own position -- `raise: false` makes any placement legal, and\nRails still runs a filter a superclass skipped before it existed; within\none class, source order decides. The superclass name is resolved the\nway Ruby does it: enclosing `module`s innermost-outward, `module\nAdmin::Deep` as ONE scope (`Module.nesting` says so), then top level,\nagainst the keys the walk actually saw, so `class Admin::X <\nApplicationController` reaches the real `application` and a wrong guess\nunder-reports a filter rather than attributing another controller''s.\nFilters are emitted above the `actions.empty?` guard because a base\ncontroller with a filter and no public actions still guards every\nsubclass. `if:`/`unless:` are ignored rather than degraded to dynamic --\na dynamic entry carries no name, so the auth heuristic could not see it\nat all -- while an unreadable `only:` becomes dynamic, since an empty\nscope would claim coverage the filter does not have. Every wire\naddition is defaulted on the Zig side, so a ZIGAPAGOS_RUNTIME_DIR older\nthan this binary decodes into empty lists, never a decode error.\n\nTwo sidecar corrections ride with the recovery because the boundary\ncannot be built without them. `resource :session` was routed to a\ncontroller \"session\"; Rails routes it to SessionsController\n(SingletonResource#controller is `options[:controller] ||\nname.to_s.pluralize`) while the path and every helper name stay\nsingular. A route whose controller does not resolve reaches no\ntemplate, raises nothing from it and produces no page, so an app''s\nwhole sign-in flow was invisible to the migration and the fixture\ncarried a `controller:` override no real app has. `Inflect.pluralize`\nis an order-preserving port of ActiveSupport''s plural rules, every\nexpectation read off a live activesupport 8.1.3.1 (`resource :person`\nis PeopleController; `series` is uncountable); routes_test.rb''s pin of\nthe wrong controller is corrected, not deleted. And a nested\n`data:`/`aria:` hash -- `link_to \"Sign out\", logout_path, data: {\nturbo_method: :delete }`, the Rails 7 spelling of a destructive link --\ncollapsed to a `data=\"{...}\"` sentinel, so no reader on the Zig side\ncould see the verb or the confirmation. `flatten_opts` now spells it\nout as ActionView''s `tag_options` does: key dasherised and\nxml-name-escaped, nil and blank keys skipped, a `button_to`''s `form: {\ndata: … }` lifted onto the control since Turbo consults it exactly as\nthe submitter''s. ActionView 8.1.3.1 parity over fourteen shapes.\n\n`Discovery` carries the new facts as deep copies (`actions`,\n`before_actions`, `skip_before_actions`, `parents`, plus a `filterSet()`\nview), for the reason `dupeRoutesForDiscovery` exists: `scaffold.write`\nreceives only `&discovery` and `discover`''s own `defer\ncontrollers.freeResult` runs first. Nothing reads them yet -- the\nfindings come next, the scaffold after that -- so no manifest or handoff\nbyte moves, and the three `emptyDiscovery` test helpers spell the\nfields out because `Discovery` declares no defaults on purpose.\n\nThe plan this stage was executed against and the spec''s \"As shipped\"\namendments ride here. Two things in the spec do not survive contact\nwith the ZigBase repository: `@zigbase/client` exports `createClient`,\n`LocalAuthStore` and `ZigbaseError`, not `ZigBase` and\n`ClientResponseError`, and its transport sends a bearer header only, so\na cookie session is unusable through it; and `x-zigbase-coverage.\nallAuthMethods` is always false, so the journey''s session endpoints can\nonly ever name client methods. The amendments say so beside the\nparagraphs they correct rather than replacing the reasoning.\n\nCloses #176\nPart of #167\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"229530527a02792f8f454d894c6266f67846915c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/229530527a02792f8f454d894c6266f67846915c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKDdlOTNkZTBiYWRhZTNiNjk0ZTA0NGIzZDgyNThkMWQ5N2YyMjU5ZmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","sha":"5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b"}],"sha":"7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/7e93de0badae3b694e044b3d8258d1d97f2259fc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T03:10:30Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T03:10:30Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Keep the manifest determinism test out of other processes'' way\n\n`manifest.zig`''s \"two runs from differently-named directories\" test\ncopied the rails-sample fixture to two FIXED paths under .zig-cache/tmp\nand `deleteTree`d them on entry and exit. The test is compiled into\nevery test binary whose module graph reaches manifest.zig -- test-init,\ntest-rails, test-migrate -- and CI runs those binaries in parallel, so\none process''s cleanup could land in the middle of another''s copy.\nIt did, on PR #183''s second CI run (33289222022): `copyTree` hit\n`FileNotFound` creating a parent that a sibling process had just\ndeleted, in the test-init binary, while the same commit''s test-rails\nrun passed. The previous push was green; nothing in between touched\nthis code.\n\nThe two copies now live inside a per-process `std.testing.tmpDir`,\nkeeping their deliberately dissimilar leaf names (the length/shape\ndifference is what the test discriminates on) while no two processes\ncan share a path.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"0e2b72e7f01e4cb126faff35bbc96791642a0264","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/0e2b72e7f01e4cb126faff35bbc96791642a0264"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKDVmZTUyM2Q2OTY0MmI1ZWQzMmI5ZWU4YTMyNjQ0YTk5YjNjY2NlMGI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5","sha":"bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5"}],"sha":"5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/5fe523d69642b5ed32b9ee8a32644a99b3ccce0b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:47:59Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:59:02Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Title MIGRATION.md by the app''s basename, not its path\n\n`report.build` printed `# Migrating {app_path} to Zigapagos` with the\npath exactly as the operator typed it, so a run from an absolute path\ncommitted `/home/<user>/src/app` into the one artifact meant to be\nchecked in beside the target. That leaks a machine-specific path and\nmakes two operators'' reports differ in bytes. Pre-existing since #166;\nflagged again by the PR #183 review.\n\n`migrate` now resolves the Rails source to its real path once, before\ndiscovery, and both consumers of the name read that: the report titles\nitself by its basename, and the scaffold''s `railsAppName` names the\nsite by the same one -- so `migrate .` no longer produces a report\ntitled `.` beside a site titled after the target directory. The target\nguard reuses the same resolved path instead of resolving a second time.\n\nA named unit test pins that an absolute path with a trailing slash\nyields the bare name and that the path itself never appears; the\npresentation e2e adds MIGRATION.md to its determinism cmp set and pins\nthe title and the absence of the scratch path. Free-text blocker\n`detail` can still quote a runtime path on a degraded run\n(`RAILS_SIDECAR_MISSING`); that channel carries no determinism contract\nand is outside this issue.\n\nCloses #178.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"5a5707f842a13f0e5602e9085befd5c73ed51aef","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/5a5707f842a13f0e5602e9085befd5c73ed51aef"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKGJjZGQzYzljYzUyZjdkNjNjMTVlY2ZhYjE2YWMxNGMwODA2YjlhZTU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7","sha":"d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7"}],"sha":"bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/bcdd3c9cc52f7d63c15ecfab16ac14c0806b9ae5"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:39:23Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:39:23Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Wire the decide-and-re-run loop into migrate, and document it\n\n`migrate <app> --from rails --target DIR [--decisions FILE]` now\nassembles the project: manifest, scaffold, handoff, then MIGRATION.md\nwith its `## Handoff` section, in that order so a scaffold failure\nleaves the manifest behind for diagnosis and the report is written\nlast, once the verdict exists. The manifest is byte-identical to the\n`-o` run''s, and the report is the `-o` report plus one section, which\ntests/migrate/rails.sh proves by splitting on the heading.\n\nExit codes are the loop''s contract. `migrate` returns the code itself\nnow rather than a bool, because Rails needs a third answer: 1 for a\nbroken discovery, an invalid decisions file or `--strict`; 3 when the\nrun wrote everything it meant to and `complete` is false; 0 when the\nmigration is done. A plain `-o` run has no verdict and never exits 3.\nBroken outranks incomplete, so an agent loop never goes off answering\nfindings for a discovery it cannot trust. `--decisions` defaults to\nDIR/MIGRATION.decisions.json, and a target that contains only that file\nis accepted -- it is the one file the operator writes into DIR between\nruns. A relative `--decisions` path is reduced to its basename before it\ncan reach a RAILS_DECISION_STALE blocker, so two operators produce the\nsame manifest bytes whatever their cwd.\n\ntests/migrate/rails-presentation.sh runs the whole loop on the fixture:\nrun 1 exits 3 with every route''s status pinned by value and the 28-file\ntarget tree pinned exactly; the checked-in MIGRATION.decisions.json\nanswers the fifteen findings that stand between it and done; run 2\nexits 0 with `complete: true`, a 21-file tree, no page for any retained\nor blocked route, the SPA declared in build.sh, and `zigapagos release`\nplus `doctor` genuinely executing on the result (the one warning --\nthe generated SPA declares no spa.head -- is pinned as a known gap so\nthe pin flips when the emitter changes). It also pins determinism, the\ninvalid-decisions exits, a stale answer, the decisions-only target rule,\nthat a Haml route cannot be marked migrated by hand, and that the source\ntree is unchanged after every run. The fixture gains a redirect-only\nroute and its decisions file; the `<%= m %>` block local it always had\nstays, because a retain answer on the route''s form now settles it.\n\ndocs/migration/rails-to-zigapagos.md (byte-mirrored into the migration\nskill) documents what `--target` writes, the conversion rules fragment\nby fragment, the decisions file and its validation errors, the handoff\nfields and `complete`, and the re-run loop -- every quoted tree, error\nstring and file body taken from a real run. Every sentence that still\nsaid the Rails importer converts nothing, in the docs, the skill, the\nsibling changelog fragments shipping in the same release and the\ngenerated MIGRATION.md itself, now says what it does instead.\n`--runtime-path` is named where it matters, since a target that answers\n`spa` does not build without it (#179).\n\nKnown gaps carried as issues rather than fixed here: MIGRATION.md\nembeds the absolute app path (#178, pre-existing), the SPA scaffold\nemits no spa.head (#180), an unmapped region with no finding cannot be\nanswered (#181), and two route-level open notes still carry no id\n(#182).\n\nPart of #167: Stage 2 of five. Stages 3-5 (backend boundary,\ninteractivity, parity) follow on their own branches, and the last of\nthem closes the issue.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"9b4a0a27226603f52d79b12beecf9220998348d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/9b4a0a27226603f52d79b12beecf9220998348d9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKGQ0NTNmNzVlYzY2YThjNzIyODkwNDk2NWQ0NWI4YTY5MzcyM2NjYTc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7","sha":"f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7"}],"sha":"d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/d453f75ec66a8c7228904965d45b8a693723cca7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:37:58Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:37:58Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Assemble the target tree and judge whether it is done\n\nWith templates convertible, this chapter writes a Zigapagos project from\na discovery plus the operator''s decisions, and produces the document\nthat says how far the migration got.\n\nscaffold.zig writes the tree: zigapagos.ziggy, a build.sh that runs the\nbinary with every SPA declared (the `--spa=` argument is quoted, because\nits `|` is a pipeline to the shell otherwise), .gitignore, the agent\nguidance files, one content/<path>/index.smd and one layouts/<ctrl>/\n<action>.shtml per convertible route under its layouts/templates/\n<layout>.shtml, the assets the manifest maps (never public/assets/**,\nwhich is the pipeline''s compiled output), and spa/<segment>.spa.tsx for\na dynamic route the operator answered `spa`. Every route ends with a\nstatus -- migrated, open, blocked, retained, backend, redirect -- and a\nnote that says why.\n\nThe order in which a route''s fate is decided is the substance here, and\nevery rule came from a review probe:\n\n- An acknowledgement is honoured first. `retain` and `blocked` settle a\n  route even when its view holds a region the converter could not map;\n  retain means the page stays on Rails, so what was unmapped is moot.\n  Only a route that would otherwise be `migrated` is held open by an\n  unmapped region, because that region carries no id a decision could\n  name.\n- A settled route writes no page. A blank <main> served for a route the\n  operator declared blocked is worse than a 404, and the handoff row is\n  the record. A view shared by several routes is converted once and\n  written by the first route that actually needs the file.\n- A route under a layout that will not convert -- Haml, Slim, a parse\n  error -- carries the layout''s own finding, so one answer settles every\n  route under it. A route whose action resolves no view carries\n  RAILS_NO_TEMPLATE. Before these, `complete` was unreachable for any\n  app with one such layout or one `render :other`.\n- A view rendered under two different layouts leaves the second route\n  open rather than serving it the first layout''s chrome.\n- Decisions on one route are ranked blocked > retain > deferred, tie on\n  the smallest id, so the outcome does not depend on file order.\n\nhandoff.zig builds MIGRATION.handoff.json (`zigapagos.rails-handoff/1`,\nschema-checked by the same rails-check gate as the manifest -- the drift\ntest''s case A now proves both schemas are watched). `complete` is\ncomputed, never asserted: every GET/HEAD route is migrated, redirect or\nbackend, or retained/blocked with a decision. A backend route needs no\npage, so it never blocks completion; Stage 3''s endpoint mapping reopens\nit through its own finding. Rows are emitted in content order with a\ndeterministic tiebreak, and no artifact carries an absolute path.\n\nreport.zig gains the `## Handoff` section that MIGRATION.md appends\nafter the discovery report: the status counts, the handoff''s own\n`complete` verdict verbatim, and what to do next -- `bash build.sh`,\nsince the script sets `-o pipefail`, which `sh` rejects.\n\nThe drift gate itself needed a fix: its EXIT-trap restore was armed\nbefore its dirty-tree refusal, so refusing to run on uncommitted edits\nalso deleted them. The trap now arms after the check.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"a52743ab451f77127fa6816e2bdf0516d27afc6d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/a52743ab451f77127fa6816e2bdf0516d27afc6d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKGY5YzAyMjRkZDVhYmFmODJjNzdiYTJjNGVjMmIxOTIyMTYzMDJjYzc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/0958709d9e0f4c9ec26a295b9337cce63298d503","sha":"0958709d9e0f4c9ec26a295b9337cce63298d503","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/0958709d9e0f4c9ec26a295b9337cce63298d503"}],"sha":"f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/f9c0224dd5abaf82c77ba2c4ec2b192216302cc7"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commits/0958709d9e0f4c9ec26a295b9337cce63298d503/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:37:07Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-30T02:37:07Z","email":"valthon@gmail.com","name":"valthon"},"message":"Convert Rails templates and read the decisions\n\nStage 1 of #167 (PR #175) made `migrate --from rails` say which\ntemplate fragments a converter would refuse; nothing yet converted. This\nchapter is the conversion engine and the two inputs it reads, kept\nstd-only under src/cli/rails/ like the discovery it extends.\n\nresolve.zig turns Rails helpers into what a Zigapagos site needs without\nguessing: `<name>_path` to a URL with each literal argument percent-\nencoded per RFC 3986 (a `*glob` keeps its slashes), an asset helper''s\nliteral to the manifest asset it names (a leading `/` is Rails'' \"already\na public path\" idiom and resolves under public/ alone), a route path to\n`content/<path>/index.smd` (always a directory index, so `/posts` and\n`/posts/:id` cannot collide on a file beside a directory), and a\ncontroller/action pair to the view Rails would render. URL-shaped\nliterals -- Rails'' own `URI_REGEXP` shapes, including `data:` -- are\nrecognised before any file lookup, because every CDN reference became a\nspurious unresolved-asset finding without that.\n\ndecisions.zig reads MIGRATION.decisions.json (`zigapagos.rails-\ndecisions/1`): every problem in the file is reported at once, an id\nthat matches no current finding is a stale answer rather than an error\n(the source moved), and a choice is validated against the finding''s own\n`choices` list so `migrated` can never be asserted by hand.\n\nconvert.zig walks a template''s node stream and emits SuperHTML. A\nlayout always declares `head` and `main` blocks and a view always fills\nthem, so the two sides agree on their block interface by construction;\n`<title>` is taken wholesale; partials are inlined at the render site.\nEvery fragment the converter will not map becomes a placeholder region:\n`<!-- rails:finding <id> -->` when a finding names it -- the id the\noperator answers -- and `<!-- rails:unmapped <kind> -->` when nothing\ndoes (a computed content_for body, an unbound block local, an\nunresolvable render). Regions nest; only the outermost one carries the\nid, so one construct is one question.\n\nfindings.zig gains the derivations the converter needs answered: form\nand form-field fragments raise RAILS_BACKEND_ENDPOINT, request-time\nstate (ivars, `current_*`, `Current`) RAILS_REQUEST_TIME_STATE, Turbo\nframes/streams and component roots their own codes, a Haml/Slim template\nRAILS_TEMPLATE_ENGINE_UNSUPPORTED as a finding beside the blocker (a\nHaml view or layout was otherwise unanswerable, and `complete` was\nunreachable for any app with one), and a GET route whose controller and\naction resolve no view RAILS_NO_TEMPLATE. Choices are a property of the\nfinding, not of its code: an ivar in a form offers a different set than\nan ivar in a heading. The engine gate is one function in inventory.zig\nshared by the blocker and the finding, so the two sets cannot drift.\n\nrails.discover now takes the decisions as an option, validates them\nagainst the findings it just derived, and reports a stale answer as a\nRAILS_DECISION_STALE blocker -- inside discover, because that blocker\nhas to reach the manifest, the report and the severity counts, all of\nwhich are written there. The one caller passes no decisions yet; the\nflag arrives with the target assembly.\n\nThe plan and the spec amendments this stage was executed against ride\nhere: the plan is deliberately leaner than Stage 1''s (exact interfaces\nand test contracts per task, internals left to the implementers), and\nthe spec now states that a backend route never blocks completion, that\ncontent paths are directory indexes, and how a Haml route reaches\nblocked or retained.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_016ceqUz8nDB4ouT3zjGBDR4","tree":{"sha":"ace0811ec2ee59e3eca8d8cb4fbb816d79928433","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/trees/ace0811ec2ee59e3eca8d8cb4fbb816d79928433"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/git/commits/0958709d9e0f4c9ec26a295b9337cce63298d503","verification":{"payload":null,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"verified":false,"verified_at":null}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5040?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/valthon","id":5040,"login":"valthon","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDA=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/valthon","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/0958709d9e0f4c9ec26a295b9337cce63298d503","node_id":"C_kwDOTkRGT9oAKDA5NTg3MDlkOWUwZjRjOWVjMjZhMjk1YjkzMzdjY2U2MzI5OGQ1MDM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/valthon/zigapagos/commit/9cccf723c7fb1d872f1a3d40c202da216b7e7a2e","sha":"9cccf723c7fb1d872f1a3d40c202da216b7e7a2e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/valthon/zigapagos/commi